00:11:53,713 --> 00:11:56,882
"let's do a send-up
of corn-pone Americana!"

249
00:11:56,966 --> 00:11:59,593
I was already writing lyrics
in my head. I couldn't stop.

250
00:11:59,677 --> 00:12:00,928
And did he call you?

251
00:12:01,011 --> 00:12:02,388
HART: Are you kidding?
He was too busy

252
00:12:02,471 --> 00:12:03,806
writing more hits. Huh.

253
00:12:05,766 --> 00:12:08,519
I sat in my box seat tonight
and watched

254
00:12:08,602 --> 00:12:10,146
"Oklahomo,

255
00:12:10,229 --> 00:12:11,647
-"exclamation point"...
-[EDDIE CHUCKLES]

256
00:12:11,731 --> 00:12:14,108
...down there, glittering
in all its pink lights

257
00:12:14,191 --> 00:12:17,403
and all those cowboy hats
and twirling lassos,

258
00:12:17,486 --> 00:12:21,949
and I knew two things
with absolute certainty.

259
00:12:22,032 --> 00:12:23,826
-[MORTY PLAYS PIANO]
-It was a 14-karat hit

260
00:12:23,909 --> 00:12:27,246
and it was a 14-karat
piece of shit.

261
00:12:27,329 --> 00:12:29,749
Oh. Friend of mine
saw it in previews,

262
00:12:29,832 --> 00:12:32,042
said it was one of
the best shows he's ever seen.

263
00:12:32,126 --> 00:12:35,963
The show is fraudulent
on every possible level.

264
00:12:36,046 --> 00:12:38,257
And this is not
jealousy speaking, okay?

265
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:41,260
I watched that show tonight

266
00:12:41,343 --> 00:12:44,847
and I felt this great sinking
in my heart.

267
00:12:44,930 --> 00:12:47,516
And all around me people
are roaring.

268
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:50,019
They are roaring
at third-rate jokes.

269
00:12:50,102 --> 00:12:52,938
I wanted to grab the audience
by the shoulders and say,

270
00:12:53,022 --> 00:12:56,776
"What are you laughing at?
Come on! Demand more!" And...

271
00:12:57,902 --> 00:12:59,987
Rodgers is a genius.

272
00:13:00,070 --> 00:13:02,656
I say that without
one second's hesitation.

273
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:04,283
You're a piano player.
You know that I'm right.

274
00:13:04,366 --> 00:13:05,367
Yeah, he's pretty good.

275
00:13:06,035 --> 00:13:07,495
Pretty good?

276
00:13:07,578 --> 00:13:11,957
There is no one with his range,
his inventiveness,

277
00:13:12,041 --> 00:13:17,505
great soaring masculine melodies
building like pile drivers.

278
00:13:17,588 --> 00:13:19,840
I mean, Rodgers is
a cold-hearted son of a bitch,

279
00:13:19,924 --> 00:13:21,592
but he can get a melody
to levitate,

280
00:13:21,675 --> 00:13:26,263
and that is the hallmark
of great art, levitation.

281
00:13:26,347 --> 00:13:31,644
♪ I've got
a beautiful feeling ♪

282
00:13:31,727 --> 00:13:33,813
That's the moment.
You hear that?

283
00:13:34,605 --> 00:13:35,981
When your spine glows

284
00:13:36,065 --> 00:13:38,234
and the whole apparatus
of songwriting

285
00:13:38,317 --> 00:13:40,444
suddenly breaks free
from gravity.

286
00:13:40,528 --> 00:13:43,322
There's maybe five people
on the planet

287
00:13:43,405 --> 00:13:44,532
that can pull that off,

288
00:13:44,615 --> 00:13:47,701
and that son of a bitch
is one of 'em.

289
00:13:47,785 --> 00:13:53,290
And then, oy... [SIGHS]
Words by Oscar Hammerstein II.

290
00:13:53,374 --> 00:13:54,750
[HART AND EDDIE CHUCKLING]

291
00:13:54,834 --> 00:13:56,794
What can I say about Oscar?

292
00:13:56,877 --> 00:13:59,129
He's going to come striding
in here any second,

293
00:13:59,213 --> 00:14:00,548
all seven
and a half feet of him,

294
00:14:00,631 --> 00:14:01,882
and, you know, Dick deliberately

295
00:14:01,966 --> 00:14:03,300
went with somebody tall
this time,

296
00:14:03,384 --> 00:14:05,135
you know that. [INHALES DEEPLY]

297
00:14:05,219 --> 00:14:06,637
But what can I say about Oscar?

298
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:10,307
He's so earthbound,

299
00:14:10,391 --> 00:14:13,727
and let's face it,
most of us are earthbound.

300
00:14:13,811 --> 00:14:16,146
But there are moments,
I swear to God,

301
00:14:16,230 --> 00:14:18,274
there are moments in my work

302
00:14:18,357 --> 00:14:20,484
when I have made something
bigger than myself.

303
00:14:20,568 --> 00:14:22,111
I agree, definitely.

304
00:14:22,194 --> 00:14:25,114
Thank you. The words were bigger
than the music,

305
00:14:25,197 --> 00:14:26,740
bigger than the characters
who sang them,

306
00:14:26,824 --> 00:14:28,325
and they approached, for maybe

307
00:14:28,409 --> 00:14:31,370
one-half second,
something immortal.

308
00:14:31,453 --> 00:14:34,498
Excuse my limitless self-regard,
but they did,

309
00:14:34,582 --> 00:14:37,376
and if nobody else is going
to say it, then I'm going to.

310
00:14:37,459 --> 00:14:39,044
I have written
a handful of words

311
00:14:39,128 --> 00:14:40,796
that are going to cheat death.

312
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:43,340
Spoken with the modesty
of a true lunatic.

313
00:14:43,424 --> 00:14:45,593
Hey! When Shakespeare wrote,

314
00:14:45,676 --> 00:14:47,970
"Not marble nor gilded monuments

315
00:14:48,053 --> 00:14:50,723
"Of princes shall outlive
this powerful rhyme,"

316
00:14:50,806 --> 00:14:53,225
did people say,
"My God, what an ego"?

317
00:14:53,309 --> 00:14:55,436
No, they said he is a genius,

318
00:14:55,519 --> 00:14:57,771
and he knows his work
is going to last.

319
00:14:57,855 --> 00:14:59,565
So now you're Shakespeare?

320
00:14:59,648 --> 00:15:01,400
Look, Oklahoma! is going to win
the goddamn Pulitzer.

321
00:15:01,483 --> 00:15:02,776
I know that.

322
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:03,694
High schools
are going to put it on

323
00:15:03,777 --> 00:15:04,987
from now till doomsday,

324
00:15:05,070 --> 00:15:08,073
because it is so inoffensive.

325
00:15:08,157 --> 00:15:11,118
But really,
who wants inoffensive art?

326
00:15:11,201 --> 00:15:12,411
Knuckles... [SNAPS FINGERS]

327
00:15:12,494 --> 00:15:13,704
...are you listening to me?

328
00:15:13,787 --> 00:15:15,039
I'm trying to answer
your question.

329
00:15:15,122 --> 00:15:16,248
Is that why God put us
on the planet,

330
00:15:16,332 --> 00:15:17,750
to not offend people?

331
00:15:17,833 --> 00:15:18,876
-[CONTINUES PLAYING TUNE]
-The problem

332
00:15:18,959 --> 00:15:20,544
with Oklahoma! is this,

333
00:15:20,628 --> 00:15:23,547
and the problem was right there
in the original play,

334
00:15:23,631 --> 00:15:25,299
Green Grow the Fucking Lilacs.

335
00:15:25,382 --> 00:15:27,968
And Dick first showed that play
to me, all right?

336
00:15:28,052 --> 00:15:30,346
He asked me to adapt it first,
and I said no,

337
00:15:30,429 --> 00:15:32,139
and I don't mind
that it's nostalgic,

338
00:15:32,222 --> 00:15:34,183
but Oklahoma!,
exclamation point,

339
00:15:34,266 --> 00:15:37,436
is nostalgic for a world
that never existed,

340
00:15:37,519 --> 00:15:40,147
and do you really
want to write a show

341
00:15:40,230 --> 00:15:42,566
where the main character's name
is Curly?

342
00:15:42,650 --> 00:15:45,235
Oh, here's an idea for a song.

343
00:15:45,319 --> 00:15:47,446
"Hey, Moe! Hey, Larry!
I Just Stepped in Cow Shit!"

344
00:15:47,529 --> 00:15:49,365
[MIMICKING THE THREE STOOGES]

345
00:15:51,075 --> 00:15:52,952
[CHUCKLES] I mean, am I bitter?

346
00:15:54,870 --> 00:15:57,915
We write together
for a quarter a century,

347
00:15:57,998 --> 00:16:01,043
and the first show
he writes with someone else

348
00:16:01,126 --> 00:16:03,963
is gonna be the biggest hit
he ever had. Am I bitter?

349
00:16:04,046 --> 00:16:05,881
-Larry...
-HART: Fuck yes.

350
00:16:05,965 --> 00:16:09,510
But I need
to tell you about Elizabeth.

351
00:16:09,593 --> 00:16:10,844
I need to cleanse my heart.

352
00:16:10,928 --> 00:16:12,471
Here we go. The 20-year-old...

353
00:16:12,554 --> 00:16:15,224
[EXHALES] She's going to come
in here any second.

354
00:16:15,307 --> 00:16:17,309
Uh, Rodgers is gonna
be here tonight, right?

355
00:16:17,393 --> 00:16:18,769
HART: Don't worry,

356
00:16:18,852 --> 00:16:20,562
-I'll introduce you.
-Oh, that'd be great.

357
00:16:20,646 --> 00:16:22,189
I'm trying to get somebody
to listen to my songs.

358
00:16:22,272 --> 00:16:24,650
Well, listen and learn.

359
00:16:24,733 --> 00:16:27,778
I'm telling you about Elizabeth.

360
00:16:28,904 --> 00:16:30,990
There's something appealingly...

361
00:16:31,907 --> 00:16:34,159
ethereal about her face.

362
00:16:34,243 --> 00:16:35,577
What the fuck does that mean?

363
00:16:35,661 --> 00:16:38,205
Look, I got her
all these presents.

364
00:16:38,288 --> 00:16:40,457
You bought her
something from Klein's?

365
00:16:40,541 --> 00:16:42,418
[CHUCKLES]
She's gonna love this.

366
00:16:42,501 --> 00:16:43,919
Yeah, but that's used shit.
What are you doing?

367
00:16:44,003 --> 00:16:45,212
HART: This is an authentic
first-edition.

368
00:16:45,295 --> 00:16:47,089
You don't understand the magic

369
00:16:47,172 --> 00:16:48,549
and the mystery of this girl.

370
00:16:48,632 --> 00:16:52,136
"I have been waiting for this
my whole life."

371
00:16:52,219 --> 00:16:53,429
It's what Somerset Maugham

372
00:16:53,512 --> 00:16:54,847
says in that thing,
you remember?

373
00:16:56,765 --> 00:16:57,766
-Me?
-HART: Yeah.

374
00:16:57,850 --> 00:16:59,768
"There's always one who loves

375
00:16:59,852 --> 00:17:01,478
"and one who lets himself
be loved."

376
00:17:02,771 --> 00:17:04,023
That's the truth, isn't it?

377
00:17:04,106 --> 00:17:05,441
Of course,
you know you're in trouble

378
00:17:05,524 --> 00:17:06,859
when you're looking
for the meaning of life

379
00:17:06,942 --> 00:17:09,028
in a book with the word
"bondage" in the title.

380
00:17:10,571 --> 00:17:11,905
Larry... [EXHALES]

381
00:17:14,783 --> 00:17:16,535
She's a girl. Right?

382
00:17:16,618 --> 00:17:18,287
Okay. See, I always thought

383
00:17:18,370 --> 00:17:20,456
that your interests
went more towards...

384
00:17:20,539 --> 00:17:21,498
HART: My interests?

385
00:17:22,958 --> 00:17:25,252
So now you're going
to analyze me?

386
00:17:25,335 --> 00:17:28,172
Thank you, Doctor Bacardi.

387
00:17:28,255 --> 00:17:30,549
Look, I met her at rehearsals
for By Jupiter.

388
00:17:30,632 --> 00:17:31,884
And she's not an actress.

389
00:17:31,967 --> 00:17:34,636
She's a college student.
Sophomore.

390
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:37,639
Yale. Not the general school,
that's for the guys.

391
00:17:37,723 --> 00:17:40,934
This is the School of Fine Arts!

392
00:17:41,018 --> 00:17:44,354
Bohemian goddessesy
in gray smocks

393
00:17:44,438 --> 00:17:47,858
mixing paint in morning light.
[CHUCKLES]

394
00:17:47,941 --> 00:17:49,818
She's all of 20 years old.

395
00:17:50,402 --> 00:17:51,570
And you're what?

396
00:17:51,653 --> 00:17:53,197
A youthful 47!

397
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:55,282
[BOTH LAUGHING]

398
00:17:55,365 --> 00:17:57,159
You know what I call her
in my letters?

399
00:17:57,242 --> 00:18:00,329
"My irreplaceable Elizabeth!"
Can you believe it?

400
00:18:00,412 --> 00:18:03,123
If I could write what's
in my heart this second,

401
00:18:03,207 --> 00:18:06,043
I would have the entire audience
levitating.

402
00:18:06,126 --> 00:18:08,170
Oklahoma! would close in shame

403
00:18:08,253 --> 00:18:10,923
if it could hear eight bars
of what's in my heart.

404
00:18:11,006 --> 00:18:12,132
And I've been
in eight bars tonight,

405
00:18:12,216 --> 00:18:13,092
so I know
what I'm talking about.

406
00:18:13,175 --> 00:18:15,177
[BANGS COUNTER] Go!

407
00:18:15,260 --> 00:18:16,762
Last summer,
I found myself awake

408
00:18:16,845 --> 00:18:18,430
at 4:30 in the morning.

409
00:18:18,514 --> 00:18:20,474
And all I had done
the previous five hours...

410
00:18:20,557 --> 00:18:23,393
What the hell are you lookin'
at your watch for?

411
00:18:23,477 --> 00:18:24,686
Larry, I'm working here.

412
00:18:24,770 --> 00:18:26,522
What the hell
do you think I'm doing?

413
00:18:26,605 --> 00:18:28,357
And I'm not even getting paid.
All right.

414
00:18:32,069 --> 00:18:33,320
TROY: More flowers...

415
00:18:33,403 --> 00:18:34,780
HART: Oh. Eddie,
get Sven another drink!

416
00:18:34,863 --> 00:18:36,323
...for Richard Rodgers.

417
00:18:36,406 --> 00:18:38,492
Jesus, how much did those cost?

418
00:18:38,575 --> 00:18:40,994
Sixteen dollars. Plus delivery.

419
00:18:42,704 --> 00:18:44,498
"Dick, I've got
a beautiful feeling,

420
00:18:44,581 --> 00:18:45,707
"everything's going your way.

421
00:18:45,791 --> 00:18:47,126
"Love, Dorothy."

422
00:18:47,209 --> 00:18:48,752
She's quoting Oscar?

423
00:18:56,051 --> 00:18:59,805
I write with her husband
for 24 years,

424
00:18:59,888 --> 00:19:02,182
and she's quoting Oscar?

425
00:19:02,266 --> 00:19:05,811
91st on the Park, The Ardsley.
We'll be going late.

426
00:19:05,894 --> 00:19:07,604
All right. Well,
thanks again, fellas.

427
00:19:09,815 --> 00:19:10,858
[DOOR OPENS]

428
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:12,734
WOMAN: Good evening.

429
00:19:17,030 --> 00:19:18,866
[PIANO CONTINUES PLAYING]

430
00:19:25,038 --> 00:19:28,167
Indulge me for one second.

431
00:19:28,250 --> 00:19:30,586
Let me recreate
for you, Elizabeth.

432
00:19:30,669 --> 00:19:33,589
Enough!
Did you screw her or not?

433
00:19:33,672 --> 00:19:36,633
Show some class, Eddie.
Knuckles, may I speak with you?

434
00:19:36,717 --> 00:19:38,135
Oh, sure, yeah.

435
00:19:38,218 --> 00:19:40,053
[SMACKS LIPS] So,
did you screw her or not?

436
00:19:45,726 --> 00:19:47,144
Elizabeth!

437
00:19:48,103 --> 00:19:49,146
Larry!

438
00:19:49,229 --> 00:19:51,773
My irreplaceable Elizabeth!

439
00:19:51,857 --> 00:19:52,858
I'm so happy to see you.

440
00:19:52,941 --> 00:19:54,443
-[KISSES]
-Ah...

441
00:19:54,526 --> 00:19:56,862
-Do you like the hair?
-HART: I love it.

442
00:19:56,945 --> 00:19:59,072
-It's much better than the red.
-I think so.

443
00:19:59,156 --> 00:20:01,450
I mean, I liked the red, too.

444
00:20:01,533 --> 00:20:03,076
But this is much more
other-worldly.

445
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:04,328
I have to go set up
for the party.

446
00:20:04,411 --> 00:20:06,455
No, no, no.
I got you some flowers!

447
00:20:06,538 --> 00:20:07,706
Aww.

448
00:20:07,789 --> 00:20:09,249
I'm overwhelmed.

449
00:20:09,333 --> 00:20:10,792
Well, I have that effect
on people.

450
00:20:10,876 --> 00:20:13,170
-I have so much to tell you.
-HART: Like what?

451
00:20:13,253 --> 00:20:14,421
I've been writing
in my journal again.

452
00:20:14,504 --> 00:20:15,881
HART: I hope you let me read it.

453
00:20:15,964 --> 00:20:17,507
No, no, no, no. The big one.

454
00:20:21,470 --> 00:20:22,596
And these are what
Richard Rodgers is getting?

455
00:20:24,181 --> 00:20:27,267
Larry, my mother would die
if she saw this.

456
00:20:27,351 --> 00:20:29,228
-With your permission.
-Permission granted.

457
00:20:31,688 --> 00:20:34,233
-That guy I told you about?
-HART: Cooper?

458
00:20:35,359 --> 00:20:36,860
-It finally happened.
-No!

459
00:20:36,944 --> 00:20:39,154
ELIZABETH: Yes.

460
00:20:39,238 --> 00:20:43,158
On my birthday.
The night of my 20th birthday.

461
00:20:43,242 --> 00:20:45,285
Pretty dramatic actually.
You could write a play about it.

462
00:20:45,369 --> 00:20:48,038
A musical. He Has Risen.

463
00:20:48,121 --> 00:20:49,915
-ELIZABETH: Larry... [CHUCKLES]
-It's an Easter musical!

464
00:20:49,998 --> 00:20:51,375
It's very religious!

465
00:20:53,043 --> 00:20:54,920
Let me clarify by saying
it almost happened.

466
00:20:55,003 --> 00:20:56,338
Clarify immediately!

467
00:20:56,421 --> 00:20:57,339
I'm gonna tell you
the whole story later.

468
00:20:57,422 --> 00:20:58,632
No, no, I demand

469
00:20:58,715 --> 00:21:01,551
to know the shorthand version
now.

470
00:21:01,635 --> 00:21:03,262
-The shorthand version? Okay.
-HART: Mmm-hmm.

471
00:21:05,472 --> 00:21:07,683
The skin on his back
was flawless.

472
00:21:08,308 --> 00:21:10,644
Gotta run.

473
00:21:10,727 --> 00:21:13,188
You're going to introduce me
to Richard Rodgers, right?

474
00:21:13,272 --> 00:21:14,606
Before the evening is over,

475
00:21:14,690 --> 00:21:16,692
you're going to get
a huge handful of Dick.

476
00:21:16,775 --> 00:21:18,360
Larry! You're so vulgar!

477
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:22,364
[CHUCKLES]
Handful of Dick, that's funny.

478
00:21:22,447 --> 00:21:23,448
-Hey...
-HART: Hmm.

479
00:21:25,993 --> 00:21:28,578
I'm writing an entire musical
about her.

480
00:21:28,662 --> 00:21:31,248
It's inspired by those
Frank Capra war pictures

481
00:21:31,331 --> 00:21:32,332
Why We Fight.

482
00:21:32,416 --> 00:21:33,542
This one will be called

483
00:21:34,293 --> 00:21:36,545
Why We Fuck.

484
00:21:36,628 --> 00:21:39,506
And the big ballad at the end
will be

485
00:21:39,589 --> 00:21:42,050
The Skin On His Back
Was Flawless.

486
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:43,176
[CHUCKLES]

487
00:21:44,261 --> 00:21:46,305
Of course, when you're 20,

488
00:21:46,388 --> 00:21:49,474
what other commodity do you have
to trade in on except your body?

489
00:21:49,558 --> 00:21:52,978
You haven't done anything,
right?

490
00:21:53,061 --> 00:21:56,273
Can you imagine what Elizabeth
would write if she saw my back?

491
00:21:57,566 --> 00:21:59,776
"The skin on his back

492
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:03,030
"looked like a white bed sheet
someone had thrown-up on.

493
00:22:03,113 --> 00:22:07,451
"His pecker looked like the horn
of a retarded unicorn."

494
00:22:07,534 --> 00:22:08,368
[EDDIE TUTTING]

495
00:22:10,537 --> 00:22:11,580
Just one...

496
00:22:12,956 --> 00:22:14,833
to get me through
congratulating him.

497
