Don't you agree?

987
00:42:18,202 --> 00:42:19,495
New Rodgers and Hart, something
people haven't seen before.

988
00:42:19,578 --> 00:42:21,205
Something big!

989
00:42:21,288 --> 00:42:22,498
Let's start
with the revival first.

990
00:42:22,581 --> 00:42:23,791
Absolutely.

991
00:42:23,874 --> 00:42:25,251
We'll take it one step
at a time...

992
00:42:25,334 --> 00:42:26,710
-Yeah, but...
-...because I really think we...

993
00:42:26,794 --> 00:42:27,753
-Hear me out.
-...can have a future together.

994
00:42:27,836 --> 00:42:30,631
Who doesn't think that?

995
00:42:30,714 --> 00:42:31,840
But I want to be
one of those composers

996
00:42:31,924 --> 00:42:35,010
who works with, uh, you know,

997
00:42:35,094 --> 00:42:36,136
other lyricists.

998
00:42:36,220 --> 00:42:37,805
Like Arlen, Kern. You love Kern.

999
00:42:37,888 --> 00:42:39,598
I love Kern.
I wanted to be Kern.

1000
00:42:39,682 --> 00:42:41,267
But we have to work
like professionals, Larry.

1001
00:42:41,350 --> 00:42:43,060
Since when haven't I worked
like a professional?

1002
00:42:43,143 --> 00:42:45,854
I want to work at specific times
in the morning, at my office.

1003
00:42:45,938 --> 00:42:48,274
-I want to adhere to a schedule.
-Yeah. That's what we do.

1004
00:42:48,357 --> 00:42:49,692
I don't want to spend any more
time looking for you, Larry.

1005
00:42:49,775 --> 00:42:50,818
Well, I'm right here.
Small as life!

1006
00:42:50,901 --> 00:42:52,278
You know what I mean.

1007
00:42:52,361 --> 00:42:53,612
I don't want to be calling up
your mother

1008
00:42:53,696 --> 00:42:55,114
at nine o'clock in the morning,
so that,

1009
00:42:55,197 --> 00:42:56,198
maybe, you might
roll out of bed at noon. Right?

1010
00:42:56,282 --> 00:42:57,491
-Dick...
-I just don't want to do

1011
00:42:57,575 --> 00:42:59,410
what we did.
That's all I'm saying.

1012
00:42:59,493 --> 00:43:01,745
It's a business, that's all.

1013
00:43:01,829 --> 00:43:03,330
Maybe I shouldn't bring it up
tonight, but I don't know,

1014
00:43:03,414 --> 00:43:04,832
-I wanted... I wanted to...
-All I want is to...

1015
00:43:04,915 --> 00:43:06,542
FAN 1: That was
your best one yet.

1016
00:43:06,625 --> 00:43:08,210
-Really.
-All right, thank you.

1017
00:43:08,294 --> 00:43:09,461
-Who is that?
-All I want is to write a show

1018
00:43:09,545 --> 00:43:10,588
we both love.

1019
00:43:13,382 --> 00:43:14,550
Yeah, I feel the same way.

1020
00:43:14,633 --> 00:43:16,927
Dick! Burns Mantle
just called it

1021
00:43:17,011 --> 00:43:19,263
"The most thoroughly
and attractively

1022
00:43:19,346 --> 00:43:20,806
"American musical comedy

1023
00:43:20,889 --> 00:43:21,890
"since Show Boat."

1024
00:43:21,974 --> 00:43:23,183
-Since Show Boat?
-Yeah.

1025
00:43:23,267 --> 00:43:25,144
Wow. That's good.

1026
00:43:25,227 --> 00:43:27,813
-FAN 2: Truly spectacular.
-Thank you, nice to see you.

1027
00:43:27,896 --> 00:43:29,648
I have to fall in love
with a show, Dick.

1028
00:43:29,732 --> 00:43:30,649
I have to want
to write that show

1029
00:43:30,733 --> 00:43:31,900
more than anything in the world,

1030
00:43:31,984 --> 00:43:34,028
to be in that audience,

1031
00:43:34,111 --> 00:43:36,030
sitting there
not missing a single word.

1032
00:43:36,113 --> 00:43:37,740
All right, well, we start with
A Connecticut Yankee,

1033
00:43:37,823 --> 00:43:39,116
four or five numbers,
and we go...

1034
00:43:39,199 --> 00:43:40,868
Okay, look.
Here's what I'm thinking.

1035
00:43:40,951 --> 00:43:42,453
The Adventures of Marco Polo.

1036
00:43:42,536 --> 00:43:44,747
Dick, Dick! Wonderful show.

1037
00:43:44,830 --> 00:43:46,749
Oh, thank you so much.
Nice to see you.

1038
00:43:46,832 --> 00:43:49,251
Just... just big.
Bigger than Jumbo. Epic.

1039
00:43:49,335 --> 00:43:51,337
A three-ring musical circus.

1040
00:43:51,420 --> 00:43:54,006
The show lasts four hours
with a dinner break!

1041
00:43:54,089 --> 00:43:58,636
It's grand comic opera.
And the stage set is the world!

1042
00:43:58,719 --> 00:44:01,305
-Uh-huh.
-We satirize everybody.

1043
00:44:01,388 --> 00:44:03,390
And you get to satirize them
musically.

1044
00:44:03,474 --> 00:44:05,809
We send up every national
musical cliche there is.

1045
00:44:05,893 --> 00:44:07,394
[IN FRENCH ACCENT]
The France number,

1046
00:44:07,478 --> 00:44:09,188
[IN ITALIAN ACCENT]
the Italian number,

1047
00:44:09,271 --> 00:44:10,939
[IN DEEP VOICE] when they get
to the Heart of Darkness,

1048
00:44:11,023 --> 00:44:13,317
the cannibals are doing
Porgy and Bess. Boom, boom.

1049
00:44:13,400 --> 00:44:15,736
♪ Bess,
you is my dinner now! ♪

1050
00:44:15,819 --> 00:44:17,071
Jesus, Larry. Come on.

1051
00:44:17,154 --> 00:44:18,113
[IN NORMAL VOICE]
This show is going

1052
00:44:18,197 --> 00:44:19,740
to have the scope of a novel.

1053
00:44:19,823 --> 00:44:21,533
It will be
the greatest challenge

1054
00:44:21,617 --> 00:44:23,327
you've ever had as a composer.

1055
00:44:23,410 --> 00:44:24,870
And what grounds it all,

1056
00:44:24,953 --> 00:44:27,164
what lets the audience
enter this story

1057
00:44:27,247 --> 00:44:28,499
is the girl.

1058
00:44:28,582 --> 00:44:30,167
-Uh-huh.
-This ethereal girl

1059
00:44:30,250 --> 00:44:32,378
whom Marco Polo has left behind.

1060
00:44:32,461 --> 00:44:34,797
She's half his age... [CHUCKLES]

1061
00:44:34,880 --> 00:44:37,549
...but it's the first time
he's felt that inkling

1062
00:44:37,633 --> 00:44:39,551
of love in years, maybe ever.

1063
00:44:39,635 --> 00:44:41,261
He swears in the first scene.

1064
00:44:41,345 --> 00:44:43,389
We set this up like Benedick
in Much Ado...

1065
00:44:43,472 --> 00:44:45,724
-Right.
-He swears that he's beyond

1066
00:44:45,808 --> 00:44:47,976
the reach of any woman.
He scorns love.

1067
00:44:48,060 --> 00:44:49,728
And we do a whole number
satirizing love songs.

1068
00:44:49,812 --> 00:44:51,939
No. Larry, I want to write shows

1069
00:44:52,022 --> 00:44:53,774
that have some emotional core
to them...

1070
00:44:53,857 --> 00:44:56,110
-Dick, great music. Great show!
-Hello, how are you?

1071
00:44:56,193 --> 00:44:57,986
Thank you. I'll take that.
You're a tough critic.

1072
00:44:58,070 --> 00:44:59,113
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

1073
00:44:59,196 --> 00:45:00,823
Remember, we established

1074
00:45:00,906 --> 00:45:02,408
in the first scene
that he's anti-love?

1075
00:45:02,491 --> 00:45:04,785
Then he meets the girl.
She's a sort of free-spirit,

1076
00:45:04,868 --> 00:45:06,328
Scandinavian-looking

1077
00:45:06,412 --> 00:45:08,789
with two freckles
on her left cheek,

1078
00:45:08,872 --> 00:45:10,374
-it's absurd. [LAUGHS]
-Don't do that.

1079
00:45:10,457 --> 00:45:13,335
She lives socially,
psychologically, sexually

1080
00:45:13,419 --> 00:45:17,005
in another universe
than he does, but he is slain.

1081
00:45:17,965 --> 00:45:20,259
He stands there paralyzed

1082
00:45:20,342 --> 00:45:23,053
in the light
of her pale green eyes.

1083
00:45:23,137 --> 00:45:26,640
This is the greatest explorer
in the history of the world,

1084
00:45:26,724 --> 00:45:28,726
and none of it
means anything to him

1085
00:45:28,809 --> 00:45:31,145
unless it's a way
to make her see him.

1086
00:45:31,228 --> 00:45:34,773
[CHUCKLES] That is
the emotional core of the story.

1087
00:45:34,857 --> 00:45:36,442
-Okay.
-We take this legendary,

1088
00:45:36,525 --> 00:45:37,693
larger-than-life man,

1089
00:45:37,776 --> 00:45:39,445
and we make him bleed.

1090
00:45:39,528 --> 00:45:41,613
Mr. Rodgers,
my name is Robert Heffner.

1091
00:45:41,697 --> 00:45:43,949
I'm the Mayor of Oklahoma City.

1092
00:45:44,032 --> 00:45:45,868
Eve and I have traveled
all the way to New York

1093
00:45:45,951 --> 00:45:47,453
-just to see the show.
-Okay. You know...

1094
00:45:47,536 --> 00:45:49,329
Welcome to New York.
We're finishing a conversation.

1095
00:45:49,413 --> 00:45:50,581
He'll be with you
in two minutes.

1096
00:45:50,664 --> 00:45:51,832
Does that sound good?

1097
00:45:51,915 --> 00:45:53,041
Two minutes,
I'll be right with you.

1098
00:45:53,125 --> 00:45:54,585
Thank you,
I hope it was worth it.

1099
00:45:54,668 --> 00:45:56,628
-It certainly was.
-Good to see you. Thank you.

1100
00:45:56,712 --> 00:45:58,213
All right, so come on.
Come on, come on, come on.

1101
00:45:58,297 --> 00:46:02,050
And they end up together?
Come on.

1102
00:46:02,134 --> 00:46:05,095
That's what the audience
is waiting around to find out!

1103
00:46:05,179 --> 00:46:06,638
That's the engine
that's driving the whole story.

1104
00:46:06,722 --> 00:46:08,098
All right, well, look, Larry,

1105
00:46:08,182 --> 00:46:09,725
if you're willing to write it,

1106
00:46:09,808 --> 00:46:11,643
really write it, and rewrite it,
and rewrite it again,

1107
00:46:11,727 --> 00:46:13,312
then, you know, it could work.

1108
00:46:13,395 --> 00:46:15,439
But I'm not gonna beg you
for this, Larry. You know?

1109
00:46:15,522 --> 00:46:16,982
We work for 15 minutes,

1110
00:46:17,065 --> 00:46:18,275
you're out the door
looking for a cigar store.

1111
00:46:18,358 --> 00:46:19,651
-It drives me...
-[LAUGHING] Okay.

1112
00:46:19,735 --> 00:46:21,236
It drives me really crazy.
I don't like it.

1113
00:46:21,320 --> 00:46:22,446
-I don't want to do it anymore.
-We've worked like that

1114
00:46:22,529 --> 00:46:24,031
-for 24 years.
-Congratulations, Dick!

1115
00:46:24,114 --> 00:46:25,407
That's exactly what we've
done for 24 years.

1116
00:46:25,491 --> 00:46:26,408
-Thank you.
-About time the Guild had

1117
00:46:26,492 --> 00:46:27,743
-a goddamned hit.
-Yeah.

1118
00:46:27,826 --> 00:46:28,744
You're absolutely,
absolutely right.

1119
00:46:28,827 --> 00:46:30,037
Anyway, I'm not going to...

1120
00:46:30,120 --> 00:46:31,163
I'm not going to fight
with you, Larry.

1121
00:46:31,246 --> 00:46:32,748
-Who's fighting?
-I don't know.

1122
00:46:32,831 --> 00:46:33,999
With a war on,

1123
00:46:34,082 --> 00:46:35,417
do audiences
really want to watch

1124
00:46:35,501 --> 00:46:36,877
cannibals singing
Porgy and Bess?

1125
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:38,879
And what do audiences
want to watch?

1126
00:46:38,962 --> 00:46:41,423
Cowboys a-whoopin'-it-up
at the box social?

1127
00:46:41,507 --> 00:46:43,008
[BOTH CHUCKLE]

1128
00:46:43,091 --> 00:46:44,635
Well, yes. Apparently.

1129
00:46:44,718 --> 00:46:46,011
Oh, that's right, I forgot.

1130
00:46:46,094 --> 00:46:47,679
Oklahoma, exclamation point,

1131
00:46:47,763 --> 00:46:49,515
addresses the urgency
of a nation at war.

1132
00:46:49,598 --> 00:46:52,935
I think every serviceman
in that audience tonight

1133
00:46:53,018 --> 00:46:54,478
thought about what
we're fighting for,

1134
00:46:54,561 --> 00:46:56,063
even for a second or two,
he thought about it.

1135
00:46:56,146 --> 00:46:57,648
And what is it
we're fighting for, exactly?

1136
00:46:57,731 --> 00:46:59,441
Feisty little girls
in gingham dresses

1137
00:46:59,525 --> 00:47:00,484
who can't say no?

1138
00:47:00,567 --> 00:47:02,152
[HART LAUGHS]

1139
00:47:02,236 --> 00:47:04,446
-Okay. All right.
-Dick, Dick, Dick. Come on.

1140
00:47:04,530 --> 00:47:06,698
Just between you and me,

1141
00:47:06,782 --> 00:47:10,035
those characters are
unrecognizable as human beings.

1142
00:47:10,118 --> 00:47:11,328
I think plenty of people
recognized them.

1143
00:47:11,411 --> 00:47:12,454
They recognized love.

1144
00:47:12,538 --> 00:47:13,831
He needs rescuing, big time.

1145
00:47:13,914 --> 00:47:15,874
They recognized, uh, family.

1146
00:47:15,958 --> 00:47:17,584
They recognized pride
in their country.

1147
00:47:17,668 --> 00:47:19,378
You're starting to sound like
Yankee Doodle Dandy.

1148
00:47:19,461 --> 00:47:21,171
I think there's significantly
more there to recognize, Larry,

1149
00:47:21,255 --> 00:47:22,756
than a bunch
of singing cannibals.

1150
00:47:22,840 --> 00:47:24,550
But cannibals are much funnier,
admit it.

1151
00:47:24,633 --> 00:47:26,260
OSCAR: Dick, you want to come
and read these!

1152
00:47:26,343 --> 00:47:27,928
Yeah, hang on! Hang on.
Be right there.

1153
00:47:28,011 --> 00:47:28,971
HART: Okay, okay, okay, okay.
Okay, okay, okay, okay.

1154
00:47:29,054 --> 00:47:30,681
I know exactly what you mean.

1155
00:47:30,764 --> 00:47:32,516
Just tell me... What do you
really want to write?

1156
00:47:32,599 --> 00:47:34,434
What's your dream show?

1157
00:47:34,518 --> 00:47:35,394
-My dream show?
-Yeah.

1158
00:47:35,477 --> 00:47:36,687
Oklahoma!

1159
00:47:40,148 --> 00:47:40,983
I mean, next.

1160
00:47:43,610 --> 00:47:46,822
Uh... We're talking about
getting the rights to Liliom.

1161
00:47:48,240 --> 00:47:49,867
-The Molnár play?
-Yeah.

1162
00:47:49,950 --> 00:47:51,577
-That was cornball in 1909.
-Yeah?

1163
00:47:51,660 --> 00:47:52,536
You really want
to write a musical

1164
00:47:52,619 --> 00:47:53,704
about a carnival barker

1165
00:47:53,787 --> 00:47:55,455
who beats up his girlfriend,

1166
00:47:55,539 --> 00:47:57,249
-dies, comes back...
-I think it'll work.

1167
00:47:57,332 --> 00:47:58,876
Yeah, and at the end,

1168
00:47:58,959 --> 00:47:59,710
all the dead people could take
off their halos and go,

1169
00:47:59,793 --> 00:48:01,420
"Oh... Purgatory!"

1170
00:48:01,503 --> 00:48:03,839
[LAUGHS] Look,
I will do it if you force me to.

1171
00:48:03,922 --> 00:48:05,132
-It's just in my heart of...
-No, I'm not forcing you to.

1172
00:48:05,215 --> 00:48:06,300
Oscar's going to do it.

1173
00:48:08,635 --> 00:48:10,053
What does Oscar know

1174
00:48:10,137 --> 00:48:11,722
about turn-of-the-century
Budapest?

1175
00:48:12,681 --> 00:48:14,683
He's going to Americanize it.

1176
00:48:15,601 --> 00:48:16,894
He's going to modernize it.

1177
00:48:17,895 --> 00:48:19,563
We're going to set it in Maine.

1178
00:48:19,646 --> 00:48:21,523
Maine? Right,
with all due respect,

1179
00:48:21,607 --> 00:48:22,858
modernizing Molnár

1180
00:48:22,941 --> 00:48:24,526
-is a deeply...
-You got to read this!

1181
00:48:24,610 --> 00:48:26,528
Is it the Times?

1182
00:48:26,612 --> 00:48:28,864
Burton Rascoe's
World-Telegram review.

1183
00:48:28,947 --> 00:48:30,198
-It's a rave.
-RODGERS: Good.

1184
00:48:30,282 --> 00:48:31,950
We'll have the Times
any moment.

1185
00:48:32,034 --> 00:48:34,077
Okay. Larry, I've got to go.
The Times is coming.

1186
00:48:34,161 --> 00:48:35,287
HART: Okay, okay.

1187
00:48:35,370 --> 00:48:37,372
-Wish me luck.
-Mazel tov!

1188
00:48:37,456 --> 00:48:38,498
As they say in Maine.

1189
00:48:39,625 --> 00:48:41,001
[HART CHUCKLES]

1190
00:48:41,084 --> 00:48:42,002
WOMAN: Hey, Dick.
That was perfect.

1191
00:48:42,085 --> 00:48:43,295
RODGERS: Oh, thank you.

1192
00:48:43,378 --> 00:48:44,713
Nice to see you.
Did you enjoy it?

1193
00:48:44,796 --> 00:48:46,506
[PLAYING PIANO]

1194
00:48:50,260 --> 00:48:51,929
[EXHALES]

1195
00:49:00,187 --> 00:49:02,147
What does Dick Rodgers
know of Maine?

1196
00:49:02,230 --> 00:49:04,358
He was bar-mitzvahed
for Christ's sake!

1197
00:49:04,441 --> 00:49:06,610
How long are they going
to keep selling that fairy tale?

1198
00:49:06,693 --> 00:49:08,028
"Have faith, have hope,

1199
00:49:08,111 --> 00:49:09,488
"the dead will arise
to comfort us!"

1200
00:49:09,571 --> 00:49:11,448
Like fuck they will.

1201
00:49:11,531 --> 00:49:13,575
Give me another shot,
Eddie. A real one.

1202
00:49:14,660 --> 00:49:16,495
[MOUTHS]

1203
00:49:16,578 --> 00:49:18,664
Join me for a shot, Andy?
Please.

1204
00:49:21,875 --> 00:49:22,960
[HART SIGHS]

1205
00:49:23,752 --> 00:49:26,046
How ya holding up?

1206
00:49:26,129 --> 00:49:27,631
HART: You know how in a marriage
they say

1207
00:49:27,714 --> 00:49:29,466
"for better or for worse"?

1208
00:49:29,549 --> 00:49:31,009
I think in terms of my life,

1209
00:49:31,093 --> 00:49:33,345
I've entered
the "for worse" part.

1210
00:49:33,428 --> 00:49:37,683
And it happened so quietly
I didn't even recognize it.

1211
00:49:37,766 --> 00:49:38,850
Have you ever felt that way?

1212
00:49:39,768 --> 00:49:41,103
Every day of my life.

1213
00:49:43,647 --> 00:49:45,232
[SOFTLY] Thank you.

1214
00:49:45,315 --> 00:49:47,192
Sometimes I think,
"Even God is finished with me."

1215
00:49:47,275 --> 00:49:48,902
I've had my hits,

1216
00:49:48,986 --> 00:49:51,863
I've loved a small,
dear collection of people.

1217
00:49:51,947 --> 00:49:53,824
And now he's turned the page.

1218
00:49:53,907 --> 00:49:56,910
[CHUCKLES] I feel superannuated.

1219
00:49:56,994 --> 00:49:59,454
"Superannuated."
That's a good thing, isn't it?

1220
00:49:59,538 --> 00:50:00,998
It means your dick doesn't work.

1221
00:50:03,083 --> 00:50:04,251
Must be why
I don't know that word.

1222
00:50:04,334 --> 00:50:05,168
HART: Hmm.

1223
00:50:08,088 --> 00:50:09,381
Put me on the list.

1224
00:50:10,549 --> 00:50:11,508
[CHUCKLES]

1225
00:50:20,475 --> 00:50:22,477
You notice the birds
are back in Central Park?

1226
00:50:23,854 --> 00:50:26,523
Hmm. What are you working
on these days?

1227
00:50:26,606 --> 00:50:29,401
Children's book.

1228
00:50:29,484 --> 00:50:31,278
Must have burned myself out
writing those essays,

1229
00:50:31,361 --> 00:50:32,195
it's not going well.

1230
00:50:33,405 --> 00:50:35,073
I think,
as far as my writing goes,

1231
00:50:35,157 --> 00:50:36,825
I've also entered
the "for worse" part.

1232
00:50:36,908 --> 00:50:38,910
HART: [CHUCKLES]
What's your story about?

1233
00:50:38,994 --> 00:50:40,245
I wish I knew.

1234
00:50:41,830 --> 00:50:43,373
The journey everybody takes?

1235
00:50:44,958 --> 00:50:48,086
Searching for what's perfect
and unattainable?

1236
00:50:48,962 --> 00:50:50,672
The ineffable!

1237
00:50:50,756 --> 00:50:53,759
Probably too elusive an idea
to put into a children's book.

1238
00:50:53,842 --> 00:50:56,678
I have a line for you.

1239
00:50:56,762 --> 00:51:00,098
"He chased her around the bed
all night before concluding

1240
00:51:00,182 --> 00:51:01,558
"she was ineffable."

1241
00:51:01,641 --> 00:51:03,727
Sure you don't mean "uneffable"?

1242
00:51:03,810 --> 00:51:05,479
[CLICKS TONGUE] Better.

1243
00:51:06,146 --> 00:51:08,315
[HART CHUCKLES]

1244
00:51:08,398 --> 00:51:09,983
You know,
I'm in the park every morning,

1245
00:51:10,067 --> 00:51:12,152
liberating this mouse
I keep catching in my kitchen.

1246
00:51:12,235 --> 00:51:15,030
-Yeah?
-I have this little glass box

1247
00:51:15,113 --> 00:51:17,824
that captures the mouse
but doesn't kill him.

1248
00:51:17,908 --> 00:51:19,785
And every morning
I walk into my kitchen,

1249
00:51:19,868 --> 00:51:21,161
and there he is,

1250
00:51:21,244 --> 00:51:22,079
at the bottom
of the refrigerator.

1251
00:51:22,162 --> 00:51:23,789
My little brown mouse,

1252
00:51:23,872 --> 00:51:26,208
happily eating the cracker
I left for him.

1253
00:51:26,291 --> 00:51:28,418
And every morning,
I carry him down to the park,

1254
00:51:28,502 --> 00:51:30,545
open the box.
He leaps out ten feet,

1255
00:51:30,629 --> 00:51:32,047
scurries into the underbrush.

1256
00:51:32,130 --> 00:51:34,800
And then the next morning,
I'll be damned,

1257
00:51:34,883 --> 00:51:37,177
he is back in the box!

1258
00:51:37,260 --> 00:51:38,762
How do you know
it's the same mouse?

1259
00:51:38,845 --> 00:51:40,430
Well, he certainly
looks the same.

1260
00:51:40,514 --> 00:51:44,101
He has that same New York look
of doomed hopefulness.

1261
00:51:45,352 --> 00:51:48,105
But how does he get up
the 19 floors?

1262
00:51:49,648 --> 00:51:50,899
Maybe he's tipping the doorman.

1263
00:51:50,982 --> 00:51:52,109
[HART CHUCKLES]

1264
00:51:53,819 --> 00:51:56,238
You know,
this morning he wasn't there.

1265
00:51:56,321 --> 00:52:00,492
Just the cracker
lying in the box.

1266
00:52:00,575 --> 00:52:02,410
I actually missed
the little fella, I really did.

1267
00:52:02,494 --> 00:52:04,704
I think I've started
to identify with him.

1268
00:52:04,788 --> 00:52:06,331
Does he have a name?

1269
00:52:07,499 --> 00:52:09,251
I'm calling him Stuart.

1270
00:52:09,334 --> 00:52:11,211
-[CHUCKLES]
-With a "W" or a "U"?

1271
00:52:12,420 --> 00:52:15,173
Hmm. A "U." Nothing fancy.

1272
00:52:15,257 --> 00:52:16,967
Just a regular
middle-class mouse.

1273
00:52:17,050 --> 00:52:19,136
[PEOPLE CHEERING]

1274
00:52:20,971 --> 00:52:23,265
I think you'll have
to excuse me.

1275
00:52:23,348 --> 00:52:26,184
All this jubilation
has gone to my bladder.

1276
00:52:29,521 --> 00:52:32,232
LANGNER: "Agnes de Mille
works small miracles

1277
00:52:32,315 --> 00:52:34,276
"and devising original dances

1278
00:52:34,359 --> 00:52:36,611
"that fit the story
and the tunes.

1279
00:52:36,695 --> 00:52:38,822
"Oklahoma! is fresh..."

1280
00:52:38,905 --> 00:52:41,074
-Okay, I got something funny.
-Hmm.

1281
00:52:41,158 --> 00:52:43,451
What do you call
a tired homosexual?

1282
00:52:43,535 --> 00:52:45,287
-[INDISTINCT CHATTER]
-I don't know.

1283
00:52:47,747 --> 00:52:48,832
Fuck, I forgot.

1284
00:52:49,749 --> 00:52:50,959
That's really funny.

1285
00:52:51,501 --> 00:52:53,211
[BOTH CHUCKLE]

1286
00:52:53,295 --> 00:52:55,046
LANGNER: "The play's
spine-tingling,

1287
00:52:55,130 --> 00:52:56,756
out of this world!"

1288
00:52:56,840 --> 00:52:58,425
[PEOPLE CHEERING]

1289
00:53:01,678 --> 00:53:02,512
[TOILET FLUSHING]

1290
00:53:04,681 --> 00:53:06,183
HART: You know,
when Atkinson wrote

1291
00:53:06,266 --> 00:53:08,059
that review for Pal Joey,
you remember that?

1292
00:53:08,894 --> 00:53:11,062
"Drab and mirthless.

1293
00:53:11,146 --> 00:53:15,525
"Can one draw sweet water
from a foul well?"

1294
00:53:15,609 --> 00:53:18,945
I cried. [CHUCKLES]
That's how hurt I was.

1295
00:53:19,029 --> 00:53:22,532
I cried
in Vivienne Segal's arms.

1296
00:53:22,616 --> 00:53:24,159
She hugged me and said,

1297
00:53:24,242 --> 00:53:27,829
"History is going
to prove him wrong, darling."

1298
00:53:27,913 --> 00:53:29,706
Wasn't that
the sweetest thing in the world

1299
00:53:29,789 --> 00:53:30,832
for someone to say?

1300
00:53:32,542 --> 00:53:34,836
And she actually calls me
"darling." Hmph.

1301
00:53:34,920 --> 00:53:36,129
[CHUCKLES SOFTLY] Wow.

1302
00:53:36,213 --> 00:53:37,672
Time magazine wrote,

1303
00:53:37,756 --> 00:53:39,841
"For those who can park
their morals in the lobby,

1304
00:53:39,925 --> 00:53:42,427
"Pal Joey is a wow."

1305
00:53:42,510 --> 00:53:44,763
What kind of piece-of-shit
praise is that?

1306
00:53:46,681 --> 00:53:48,475
[SNIFFLES] I'm talking
about Pal Joey.

1307
00:53:48,558 --> 00:53:49,684
Right.

1308
00:53:49,768 --> 00:53:51,394
I didn't see that one.

1309
00:53:51,478 --> 00:53:55,482
Wolcott Gibbs,
he loved the show.

1310
00:53:55,565 --> 00:53:58,026
He said it was,
"A song and dance production

1311
00:53:58,109 --> 00:54:01,529
"with living
three-dimensional figures,

1312
00:54:01,613 --> 00:54:05,242
"talking and behaving
like human beings."

1313
00:54:05,325 --> 00:54:07,244
-Ah, see? There you go.
-Mmm-hmm.

1314
00:54:08,620 --> 00:54:09,663
You're still going
to introduce me

1315
00:54:09,746 --> 00:54:11,122
to Rodgers tonight, right?

1316
00:54:11,206 --> 00:54:14,042
Urination for me
has turned into a two-act play

1317
00:54:14,125 --> 00:54:15,460
with a brief intermission.

1318
00:54:15,543 --> 00:54:17,254
[BOTH LAUGHING]

1319
00:54:19,089 --> 00:54:23,468
Oh, I love Vivienne.
God help me. [LAUGHS]

1320
00:54:24,219 --> 00:54:26,721
Her compassion.

1321
00:54:26,805 --> 00:54:29,224
Oh, did I tell you
she calls me "darling"?

1322
00:54:29,307 --> 00:54:31,559
We were up
at the Starlight Roof.

1323
00:54:31,643 --> 00:54:32,978
I turned to her and I said,

1324
00:54:33,061 --> 00:54:36,773
"Vivienne, will you
please marry me?"

1325
00:54:36,856 --> 00:54:39,526
That's what I told her.
Right to her face.

1326
00:54:39,609 --> 00:54:41,528
Can you imagine
having the courage

1327
00:54:41,611 --> 00:54:43,989
to say that to a woman?

1328
00:54:44,072 --> 00:54:46,157
I've never had the courage
to even dream of saying that.

1329
00:54:46,241 --> 00:54:47,909
Mmm-hmm. Yeah. It was a moment.

1330
00:54:47,993 --> 00:54:50,578
Let me tell you. 2:00 a.m.,
the band was playing

1331
00:54:50,662 --> 00:54:53,498
I've Heard That Song Before.

1332
00:54:53,581 --> 00:54:56,459
She said, "I love you, darling,

1333
00:54:57,377 --> 00:54:58,837
"just not that way."

1334
00:55:01,131 --> 00:55:03,425
"Not that way."

1335
00:55:04,175 --> 00:55:06,970
Three little words.

1336
00:55:07,053 --> 00:55:11,182
Ten little letters that mean,

1337
00:55:11,266 --> 00:55:14,060
"Game over, schmuck." [LAUGHS]

1338
00:55:15,061 --> 00:55:17,814
Ah, "Think it over," I told her.

1339
00:55:17,897 --> 00:55:21,901
I took her hand in mine,
her nail polish was pale gray...

1340
00:55:21,985 --> 00:55:23,028
[DOOR CLOSES]

1341
00:55:27,866 --> 00:55:31,745
-[INDISTINCT CHATTER]
-Now... I can drink.

1342
00:55:31,828 --> 00:55:34,748
[ALL CHEERING]

1343
00:55:34,831 --> 00:55:36,875
May I remind everybody
what Mike Todd said

1344
00:55:36,958 --> 00:55:38,543
about Oklahoma! in New Haven,

1345
00:55:38,626 --> 00:55:40,295
"No laughs, no tits,

1346
00:55:40,378 --> 00:55:42,005
-"no chance"?
-[ALL LAUGHING]

1347
00:55:42,088 --> 00:55:43,340
We have a chance now.

1348
00:55:43,423 --> 00:55:46,051
All right, read that out.
Okay, wow.

1349
00:55:46,134 --> 00:55:49,137
LANGNER: Wonderful. This is what
Nichols said in the Times.

1350
00:55:49,220 --> 00:55:51,514
"Wonderful
is the nearest adjective,

1351
00:55:51,598 --> 00:55:53,600
"for this excursion of the Guild

1352
00:55:53,683 --> 00:55:56,186
"combines a fresh
and infectious gaiety,

1353
00:55:56,269 --> 00:55:57,562
"a charm of manner,

1354
00:55:57,645 --> 00:56:00,065
"beautiful acting,
dancing and singing,

1355
00:56:00,148 --> 00:56:02,984
"and a score by Richard Rodgers

1356
00:56:03,068 --> 00:56:04,444
which doesn't do
any harm either,

1357
00:56:04,527 --> 00:56:06,905
"since it is one of his best.

1358
00:56:06,988 --> 00:56:09,032
[ALL CHEERING]

1359
00:56:12,577 --> 00:56:16,206
"Mr. Rodgers's scores
never lack grace,

1360
00:56:16,289 --> 00:56:19,459
"but seldom have they been
so well-integrated

1361
00:56:19,542 --> 00:56:21,628
"as this for Oklahoma!

1362
00:56:21,711 --> 00:56:24,798
"He has turned out waltzes,
love songs,

1363
00:56:24,881 --> 00:56:27,759
"comic songs and a title number

1364
00:56:27,842 --> 00:56:31,096
"which the State in question
would do well to adopt

1365
00:56:31,179 --> 00:56:32,722
"as its anthem forthwith.

1366
00:56:32,806 --> 00:56:34,557
[PEOPLE CHEERING]

1367
00:56:37,394 --> 00:56:39,229
" Oh, What a Beautiful Morning,

1368
00:56:39,312 --> 00:56:41,564
"and People Will Say
We're in Love

1369
00:56:41,648 --> 00:56:44,859
"are headed for countless
jukeboxes across the land.

1370
00:56:44,943 --> 00:56:48,071
[ALL CHEERING]

1371
00:56:48,154 --> 00:56:52,992
"And a dirge called 'Pore Jud',
in which the hero of the fable

1372
00:56:53,076 --> 00:56:56,204
"tries to persuade his rival
to hang himself

1373
00:56:56,287 --> 00:56:59,040
-is amazingly comic.
-[ALL LAUGHING]

1374
00:56:59,124 --> 00:57:01,418
"The Farmer and the Cowman

1375
00:57:01,501 --> 00:57:03,628
"and The Surrey
with the Fringe on the Top

1376
00:57:03,711 --> 00:57:05,713
"also deserve mention

1377
00:57:05,797 --> 00:57:09,843
"only because they quite clearly
approach perfection."

1378
00:57:09,926 --> 00:57:11,219
-[ALL APPLAUDING]
-Read that part again.

1379
00:57:13,012 --> 00:57:16,474
"...because they quite clearly
approach perfection."

1380
00:57:16,558 --> 00:57:18,685
Put that on the marquee!

1381
00:57:18,768 --> 00:57:21,312
"Quite clearly
approaches perfection."

1382
00:57:21,396 --> 00:57:23,231
[ALL CHEERING]

1383
00:57:24,566 --> 00:57:25,900
Come on, everybody!

1384
00:57:25,984 --> 00:57:28,653
Party upstairs!
Cake and champagne.

1385
00:57:28,736 --> 00:57:30,155
[ALL CHEERING]

1386
00:57:30,238 --> 00:57:31,698
[PIANO MUSIC PLAYING]

1387
00:57:31,781 --> 00:57:33,533
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

1388
00:57:39,164 --> 00:57:41,583
Oh, Oscar! Bravo, bravo.

1389
00:57:41,666 --> 00:57:43,460
Congratulations. Oh...
[HESITATING]

1390
00:57:49,174 --> 00:57:50,550
Can I speak with you
for a moment?

1391
00:57:50,633 --> 00:57:51,676
Can we talk later, Larry?

1392
00:57:51,759 --> 00:57:53,261
No, no, no. It's just a second.

1393
00:57:53,344 --> 00:57:54,554
I have something
important to say.

1394
00:57:54,637 --> 00:57:56,014
Just a moment
with the musical genius.

1395
00:57:56,097 --> 00:57:57,348
[HART CHUCKLES]

1396
00:57:58,975 --> 00:58:02,103
I just have one thing
I want to tell you, Dick.

1397
00:58:02,187 --> 00:58:06,774
Uh... Just one second, Dick.
It's important.

1398
00:58:06,858 --> 00:58:08,735
I remember
when I first heard about you,

1399
00:58:08,818 --> 00:58:11,404
you were just Morty Rodger's
little brother.

1400
00:58:11,488 --> 00:58:13,406
-What, were you 17?
-16.

1401
00:58:13,490 --> 00:58:15,158
-Yeah, I was 23.
-Swell job.

1402
00:58:15,241 --> 00:58:16,701
Yeah, you were the wise old man
on the mountain.

1403
00:58:16,784 --> 00:58:18,870
But when I first heard you
play your stuff,

1404
00:58:18,953 --> 00:58:21,706
I knew you had it.

1405
00:58:21,789 --> 00:58:23,666
I wasn't entirely convinced
that I had it,

1406
00:58:23,750 --> 00:58:26,127
but I heard something
that afternoon.

1407
00:58:28,338 --> 00:58:31,508
Originality, melody, grace...

1408
00:58:33,009 --> 00:58:34,552
-Oh... [BREATH TREMBLING]
-[LAUGHS]

1409
00:58:34,636 --> 00:58:36,679
Come on.
Come on, Larry. Stop it.

1410
00:58:36,763 --> 00:58:38,264
Come on, what's the matter
with you?

1411
00:58:38,348 --> 00:58:41,976
You worked your whole life
for this night, Dick.

1412
00:58:42,060 --> 00:58:44,938
Nobody's worked harder.
And nobody deserves it more.

1413
00:58:45,021 --> 00:58:46,898
All right, thanks.
Thanks, Larry. Thank you.

1414
00:58:46,981 --> 00:58:49,442
All right, that's
what I wanted... Just go...

1415
00:58:49,526 --> 00:58:51,277
[SNIFFLES AND LAUGHS]
...enjoy your party.

1416
00:58:51,361 --> 00:58:53,029
-Hey, look at me. Look at me.
-Mmm-hmm?

1417
00:58:53,112 --> 00:58:55,448
We're going to do
A Connecticut Yankee, all new.

1418
00:58:55,532 --> 00:58:56,324
-Yeah, yeah.
-We're gonna write

1419
00:58:56,407 --> 00:58:57,492
four or five new songs.

1420
00:58:57,575 --> 00:58:59,244
-I have ideas already.
-Right?

1421
00:58:59,327 --> 00:59:01,329
Yeah, yeah. And if I get some
pages down for Marco Polo,

1422
00:59:01,412 --> 00:59:02,497
can I send them over?

1423
00:59:03,915 --> 00:59:05,250
You have to ask me that?

1424
00:59:05,333 --> 00:59:07,710
Larry, I owe
my professional life to you.

1425
00:59:11,673 --> 00:59:13,466
♪ Summer journeys
to Niagara ♪

1426
00:59:13,550 --> 00:59:15,051
♪ And to other places ♪

1427
00:59:15,134 --> 00:59:18,096
♪ Aggravate all our cares... ♪

1428
00:59:18,179 --> 00:59:19,806
You remember we heard that
on the radio for the first time?

1429
00:59:19,889 --> 00:59:22,642
In your parents' living room.
Our first hit.

1430
00:59:22,725 --> 00:59:23,977
That was one of the most
astonishing moments

1431
00:59:24,060 --> 00:59:25,144
of my life, Larry.

1432
00:59:25,228 --> 00:59:26,521
I was 22.

1433
00:59:26,604 --> 00:59:29,315
And I'm telling you,
I swear to God,

1434
00:59:29,399 --> 00:59:31,734
our best work
is still ahead of us.

1435
00:59:31,818 --> 00:59:33,820
Yes, the new
Connecticut Yankee!

1436
00:59:33,903 --> 00:59:35,613
Yeah, yeah. And bigger stuff.

1437
00:59:35,697 --> 00:59:37,490
-MAN: Dick, get up here.
-All right. I'm coming!

1438
00:59:37,574 --> 00:59:40,535
I mean, Marco Polo is going
to be a show about joy...

1439
00:59:40,618 --> 00:59:45,081
but a hard-earned joy,
an unsentimental joy.

1440
00:59:45,164 --> 00:59:46,249
Something wrong
with sentimental?

1441
00:59:46,332 --> 00:59:47,417
What? It's too easy.

1442
00:59:50,628 --> 00:59:52,922
-Oklahoma! is too easy?
-[SPUTTERS]

1443
00:59:53,006 --> 00:59:54,257
The guy actually
getting the girl

1444
00:59:54,340 --> 00:59:56,009
at the end is too easy?

1445
00:59:56,092 --> 00:59:58,177
You've just eliminated every
successful musical comedy...

1446
00:59:58,261 --> 00:59:59,387
-[CHUCKLES]
-...ever written, Larry.

1447
00:59:59,470 --> 01:00:00,680
Then it's too easy for me.

1448
01:00:00,763 --> 01:00:01,598
Did you hear
the audience tonight?

1449
01:00:02,307 --> 01:00:04,517
Yes.

1450
01:00:04,601 --> 01:00:06,853
Sixteen hundred people
didn't think it was too easy.

1451
01:00:06,936 --> 01:00:08,646
You're telling me
1,600 people are wrong?

1452
01:00:08,730 --> 01:00:10,023
I'm just saying...

1453
01:00:11,357 --> 01:00:14,694
that you and I can do something

1454
01:00:14,777 --> 01:00:17,405
so much more
emotionally complicated.

1455
01:00:17,488 --> 01:00:19,657
We don't have to pander
to what audiences...

1456
01:00:19,741 --> 01:00:21,701
-Oscar and I are pandering?
-No. I didn't say that.

1457
01:00:21,784 --> 01:00:23,786
-Irving Berlin is pandering?
-I love Berlin.

1458
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:25,163
White Christmas is pandering?

1459
01:00:25,246 --> 01:00:26,456
Well, I don't
believe White Christmas.

1460
01:00:26,539 --> 01:00:28,791
-Okay.
-[BOTH CHUCKLE]

1461
01:00:28,875 --> 01:00:30,460
Well, maybe audiences
have changed.

1462
01:00:30,543 --> 01:00:32,462
Well, they still want to laugh.

1463
01:00:32,545 --> 01:00:34,172
They want to laugh,
but not in that way.

1464
01:00:34,255 --> 01:00:36,924
-Not in what way?
-In your way.

1465
01:00:37,008 --> 01:00:38,676
They want to laugh, but they
also want to cry a little.

1466
01:00:38,760 --> 01:00:41,471
They want to...
they want to feel.

1467
01:00:41,554 --> 01:00:42,639
HART: Hmm.

1468
01:00:44,807 --> 01:00:46,768
You're my oldest friend.

1469
01:00:46,851 --> 01:00:49,354
-[SIGHS WEARILY]
-[HESITATING] You're...

1470
01:00:50,647 --> 01:00:52,774
-you're unique.
-Hmm.

1471
01:00:52,857 --> 01:00:55,151
Sounds like you're
writing my obituary.

1472
01:00:55,234 --> 01:00:58,112
-I'm just saying.
-I'm right here, right now.

1473
01:00:58,196 --> 01:01:01,115
-Ready to work.
-Yeah?

1474
01:01:01,199 --> 01:01:02,742
HART: Hmm.

1475
01:01:02,825 --> 01:01:05,912
And you're willing to go
back to Doctors Hospital?

1476
01:01:05,995 --> 01:01:08,706
I don't need to go back
to Doctors Hospital,

1477
01:01:08,790 --> 01:01:11,751
and I don't need
a psychiatrist either.

1478
01:01:11,834 --> 01:01:13,169
Thank you very much.

1479
01:01:13,252 --> 01:01:14,379
We were just trying
to help you, Larry.

1480
01:01:14,462 --> 01:01:16,047
Who's we?
You and Oscar? Help me?

1481
01:01:16,130 --> 01:01:17,882
Oscar? You think Oscar
had anything to do with it?

1482
01:01:17,965 --> 01:01:20,927
Oklahoma! is going to be
the biggest hit of your career.

1483
01:01:21,010 --> 01:01:22,470
You don't know that.
You don't know that.

1484
01:01:22,553 --> 01:01:24,263
You got that asshole Kron
to manage my money.

1485
01:01:24,347 --> 01:01:25,848
Now I'm incapable...

1486
01:01:25,932 --> 01:01:27,100
I did that at the insistence
of your brother, Larry.

1487
01:01:27,183 --> 01:01:28,726
Teddy literally begged me...

1488
01:01:28,810 --> 01:01:30,353
Well, maybe you and Teddy
should write the lyrics.

1489
01:01:30,436 --> 01:01:32,105
I did write the lyrics.

1490
01:01:32,188 --> 01:01:34,315
-[LAUGHS SARCASTICALLY]
-What is that supposed to mean?

1491
01:01:34,399 --> 01:01:37,110
By Jupiter? Have you
conveniently blocked that out?

1492
01:01:37,193 --> 01:01:38,444
Blocked what out?

1493
01:01:38,528 --> 01:01:39,987
How Logan and I begged you

1494
01:01:40,071 --> 01:01:42,031
for extra choruses,
extra verses,

1495
01:01:42,115 --> 01:01:44,992
and you were so drunk
that you didn't even show up.

1496
01:01:45,076 --> 01:01:46,869
So, yes,
I had to write the lyrics.

1497
01:01:46,953 --> 01:01:48,538
You didn't write
one fucking word...

1498
01:01:48,621 --> 01:01:50,081
How would you know?

1499
01:01:50,164 --> 01:01:51,541
-...of that show.
-How would you know, Larry?

1500
01:01:51,624 --> 01:01:53,251
You weren't around to even...

1501
01:01:53,334 --> 01:01:54,794
You know what?

1502
01:01:54,877 --> 01:01:56,421
I'm actually not going to argue
with you tonight,

1503
01:01:56,504 --> 01:01:57,422
-if that's all right with you.
-I like to argue.

1504
01:01:57,505 --> 01:01:59,340
Well I don't. I don't.

1505
01:01:59,424 --> 01:02:00,633
Look, I am sorry.

1506
01:02:01,884 --> 01:02:04,387
I don't care
if somebody attacks me.

1507
01:02:04,470 --> 01:02:06,764
It doesn't mean anything to me.

1508
01:02:06,848 --> 01:02:10,601
But nobody can attack my work.
It is all I've got.

1509
01:02:10,685 --> 01:02:12,395
Your work is brilliant.
That's not the problem.

1510
01:02:15,606 --> 01:02:18,735
Your work has been brilliant
since the day I met you.

1511
01:02:20,111 --> 01:02:22,572
Do you still remember that?

1512
01:02:22,655 --> 01:02:23,781
You, standing there
in your carpet slippers

1513
01:02:23,865 --> 01:02:24,907
and your stripy bathrobe?

1514
01:02:24,991 --> 01:02:26,951
[HART CHUCKLES]

1515
01:02:27,034 --> 01:02:31,122
Yeah, with the five o'clock
shadow at 11:00 a.m.

1516
01:02:31,205 --> 01:02:33,624
[BOTH CHUCKLING]

1517
01:02:33,708 --> 01:02:36,627
I'll remember that
till the last day of my life.

1518
01:02:39,547 --> 01:02:41,174
[INHALES DEEPLY] Okay.

1519
01:02:44,177 --> 01:02:45,386
[SIGHS]

1520
01:02:46,846 --> 01:02:48,473
[LAUGHS]

1521
01:02:48,556 --> 01:02:50,057
-Larry, I got to go upstairs.
-I know.

1522
01:02:50,141 --> 01:02:51,684
-The whole company's waiting.
-Of course. Of course.

1523
01:02:53,352 --> 01:02:55,021
Four or five songs, yes?

1524
01:02:55,104 --> 01:02:56,773
I'm telling you,
I'm having ideas already!

1525
01:02:57,940 --> 01:02:59,984
A big comedy solo for Vivienne

1526
01:03:00,067 --> 01:03:01,611
about how she's killed off

1527
01:03:01,694 --> 01:03:03,780
every single
one of her previous husbands.

1528
01:03:05,156 --> 01:03:06,449
Might be funny.

1529
01:03:06,532 --> 01:03:08,826
We can call it
To Keep Our Love Alive!

1530
01:03:22,131 --> 01:03:22,965
[GIGGLES]

1531
01:03:24,133 --> 01:03:25,802
-You okay, Larry?
-Mmm-hmm.

1532
01:03:30,556 --> 01:03:31,766
What happened to White?

1533
01:03:31,849 --> 01:03:34,852
-Left.
-"So soon?"

1534
01:03:34,936 --> 01:03:37,230
There was so much more
I wanted to say to him.

1535
01:03:37,313 --> 01:03:39,023
-Maybe he was an extra, too.
-[SIGHS SOFTLY]

1536
01:03:40,149 --> 01:03:41,025
One more?

1537
01:03:42,026 --> 01:03:42,985
Last one, Larry.

1538
01:03:45,238 --> 01:03:47,824
Do you know
the ineffectual blues?

1539
01:03:50,201 --> 01:03:52,286
That's because
I haven't written it yet.

1540
01:03:52,370 --> 01:03:55,206
[CHUCKLES]

1541
01:03:55,289 --> 01:03:58,751
[SIGHS] They should put
my picture on that bottle.

1542
01:03:58,835 --> 01:04:02,672
"The Whiskey That Made
Lorenz Hart Unemployable."

1543
01:04:02,755 --> 01:04:04,674
[SCOFFS] "Buy War Bonds."

1544
01:04:09,762 --> 01:04:11,305
[EXHALES]

1545
01:04:11,389 --> 01:04:14,058
You know, I've started
to hear things wrong.

1546
01:04:14,141 --> 01:04:16,894
The other night, I was listening
to this singer go on

1547
01:04:16,978 --> 01:04:19,105
about "her cigarette heart."

1548
01:04:19,188 --> 01:04:22,066
And I thought, "Now that
is an original metaphor,

1549
01:04:22,149 --> 01:04:24,485
"'My cigarette heart.'"

1550
01:04:24,569 --> 01:04:27,780
Then I realized she was singing
"My secret heart."

1551
01:04:27,864 --> 01:04:30,741
I can't remember
anything anymore either.

1552
01:04:30,825 --> 01:04:33,160
That should be the title
of my autobiography,

1553
01:04:33,244 --> 01:04:36,372
"Stop Me
If I've Told You This Already."

1554
01:04:36,455 --> 01:04:39,125
And we'll print the entire text
twice. [CHUCKLES SOFTLY]

1555
01:04:45,882 --> 01:04:48,092
ELIZABETH: No, I'm serious.
I've seen her do it before.

1556
01:04:48,175 --> 01:04:49,594
GEORGE: As long as there's
no dancing

1557
01:04:49,677 --> 01:04:50,887
later on in the night.

1558
01:04:51,554 --> 01:04:52,847
[CHUCKLING]

1559
01:04:52,930 --> 01:04:54,348
ELIZABETH:
You better watch yourself...

1560
01:04:54,432 --> 01:04:57,226
My irreplaceable Elizabeth!

1561
01:04:57,310 --> 01:04:58,144
Larry!

1562
01:04:59,270 --> 01:05:01,147
-Do you like it?
-HART: It's...

1563
01:05:01,230 --> 01:05:03,774
Oscar Hammerstein
just said I was a gem!

1564
01:05:03,858 --> 01:05:07,361
He has such a way with words.

1565
01:05:07,445 --> 01:05:09,363
George, this is Lorenz Hart.

1566
01:05:09,447 --> 01:05:11,699
I am legitimately honored
to meet you.

1567
01:05:11,782 --> 01:05:13,117
Would you mind if I spoke

1568
01:05:13,200 --> 01:05:15,286
to Elizabeth alone
for two minutes?

1569
01:05:15,369 --> 01:05:16,954
And then she's all yours.

1570
01:05:17,038 --> 01:05:18,664
It would be perfectly all right

1571
01:05:18,748 --> 01:05:21,125
as long as you promise me
your autograph before I go.

1572
01:05:21,208 --> 01:05:22,752
ELIZABETH: George is a senior
at Yale.

1573
01:05:22,835 --> 01:05:25,171
He's studying music,
but he wants to be a director.

1574
01:05:25,254 --> 01:05:26,547
A director.
Well, what's your name?

1575
01:05:26,631 --> 01:05:28,341
George Hill.

1576
01:05:28,424 --> 01:05:31,677
Here's my advice to you,
future director, George Hill.

1577
01:05:31,761 --> 01:05:33,095
Do you want my advice?

1578
01:05:33,179 --> 01:05:33,971
I'd be honored.

1579
01:05:35,264 --> 01:05:38,559
Be careful of love stories.

1580
01:05:38,643 --> 01:05:41,395
Think about friendship stories.

1581
01:05:41,479 --> 01:05:46,108
That's where
the really enduring stuff lives.

1582
01:05:46,192 --> 01:05:47,985
-Thank you. I'll remember that.
-HART: Hmm!

1583
01:05:51,364 --> 01:05:52,823
Catch up with you later, George.

1584
01:05:55,368 --> 01:05:56,869
My mother just gave me this.

1585
01:05:56,953 --> 01:05:58,371
I was really touched
she gave it to me.

1586
01:05:59,705 --> 01:06:01,666
My well-tempered clavicle.

1587
01:06:01,749 --> 01:06:03,250
Careful. My mother...

1588
01:06:04,168 --> 01:06:05,920
[SOFTLY] Okay. All right...

1589
01:06:06,003 --> 01:06:10,174
Sorry. Now, I know your birthday
isn't until November 19th.

1590
01:06:10,257 --> 01:06:11,467
I'm a scorpion.

1591
01:06:11,550 --> 01:06:14,220
I never doubted that
for a second.

1592
01:06:14,303 --> 01:06:16,472
Did you get the sheet music
I sent you?

1593
01:06:16,555 --> 01:06:18,683
Ah, I was so busy with exams,

1594
01:06:18,766 --> 01:06:20,685
I put them away,
honestly, and...

1595
01:06:20,768 --> 01:06:22,103
And then I kind of forgot
about them.

1596
01:06:22,186 --> 01:06:24,146
But now I've got the time.
I promise you.

1597
01:06:24,230 --> 01:06:25,856
I'm going
to get a friend of mine

1598
01:06:25,940 --> 01:06:27,483
to play every single one
of those songs for me.

1599
01:06:27,566 --> 01:06:29,443
Take your time.

1600
01:06:29,527 --> 01:06:31,779
That's the wonderful thing
about art, isn't it?

1601
01:06:31,862 --> 01:06:34,198
It waits for you.

1602
01:06:34,281 --> 01:06:36,242
Thank you for all the letters
you wrote me.

1603
01:06:36,325 --> 01:06:38,077
I really enjoyed reading them.

1604
01:06:38,160 --> 01:06:39,620
My roommate couldn't believe

1605
01:06:39,704 --> 01:06:41,372
I was actually corresponding
with Lorenz Hart.

1606
01:06:41,455 --> 01:06:44,000
I remember in Vermont,
you were drinking red wine.

1607
01:06:45,042 --> 01:06:48,671
Malbec. It's a big Yale drink.

1608
01:06:48,754 --> 01:06:52,466
You ask for the French Malbec,
and then you act

1609
01:06:52,550 --> 01:06:54,510
as if you can actually tell
which vineyard it came from.

1610
01:06:54,593 --> 01:06:56,971
-[BOTH CHUCKLE]
-Oh...

1611
01:06:57,054 --> 01:06:59,682
Don't mention Vermont
around my mother, okay?

1612
01:06:59,765 --> 01:07:01,267
She doesn't know anything
about our little getaway.

1613
01:07:02,643 --> 01:07:04,603
Actually, thank goodness.

1614
01:07:04,687 --> 01:07:07,773
She doesn't know about 90%
of what's going on in my life.

1615
01:07:07,857 --> 01:07:10,359
Well, I feel privileged
that I do.

1616
01:07:10,443 --> 01:07:13,654
Or that I did, back in August.

1617
01:07:13,738 --> 01:07:16,282
I've got much more stuff to
report on since then. [LAUGHS]

1618
01:07:16,365 --> 01:07:19,827
Well, tell me about the guys.
That's what I'm interested in.

1619
01:07:19,910 --> 01:07:23,080
[CLICKS TONGUE] Honestly,

1620
01:07:23,164 --> 01:07:24,707
I'm much smarter
than most of them.

1621
01:07:24,790 --> 01:07:26,125
So I sort of sit back

1622
01:07:26,208 --> 01:07:28,044
and watch them
trying to impress me.

1623
01:07:28,127 --> 01:07:29,712
It's pretty entertaining.

1624
01:07:29,795 --> 01:07:30,921
If I were a college guy,

1625
01:07:31,005 --> 01:07:32,339
I think
I would be afraid of you.

1626
01:07:32,423 --> 01:07:35,301
This intelligent,
mercilessly observant,

1627
01:07:35,384 --> 01:07:37,970
wickedly unsentimental beauty
sitting there,

1628
01:07:38,054 --> 01:07:40,931
evaluating me from somewhere
behind those green eyes,

1629
01:07:41,015 --> 01:07:42,683
annotating all my gaucheries.

1630
01:07:42,767 --> 01:07:44,643
My God, I would be terrified.

1631
01:07:44,727 --> 01:07:46,312
Hey, I'm not that intimidating.

1632
01:07:47,772 --> 01:07:50,066
Tell me more about this Cooper.

1633
01:07:52,401 --> 01:07:53,569
This is embarrassing. [LAUGHS]

1634
01:07:53,652 --> 01:07:55,112
I love it
when you're embarrassed,

1635
01:07:55,196 --> 01:07:57,782
you get this scarlet blush
that washes up over you,

1636
01:07:57,865 --> 01:08:00,326
and a second later it's gone.

1637
01:08:00,409 --> 01:08:02,620
This one might last
considerably longer.

1638
01:08:02,703 --> 01:08:04,789
-I'd love to...
-Elizabeth. Hello, Larry.

1639
01:08:04,872 --> 01:08:07,958
I hope I'm not interrupting
anything too weighty.

1640
01:08:08,042 --> 01:08:10,086
Well, weighty affairs
will have to wait.

1641
01:08:10,961 --> 01:08:12,630
She's a gem, isn't she?

1642
01:08:12,713 --> 01:08:14,215
Please, Mr. Hammerstein.

1643
01:08:14,298 --> 01:08:15,966
I'll only bother you
for a moment.

1644
01:08:16,050 --> 01:08:18,719
Larry, I just wanted to say...

1645
01:08:18,803 --> 01:08:20,304
Well, firstly, I recognize

1646
01:08:20,387 --> 01:08:22,056
this must be
a difficult night for you.

1647
01:08:22,139 --> 01:08:25,309
No, I'm a professional, Oscar.
We're both professionals,

1648
01:08:25,392 --> 01:08:27,353
we understand the nature
of our business.

1649
01:08:27,436 --> 01:08:30,231
Larry, did you really
like the show?

1650
01:08:30,314 --> 01:08:31,899
I don't think I do
the comedy numbers

1651
01:08:31,982 --> 01:08:34,193
half as well as you do.
They're not my strength.

1652
01:08:34,276 --> 01:08:37,947
I laughed my ass off.
I had to have it re-attached.

1653
01:08:38,030 --> 01:08:39,657
[BOTH CHUCKLE]

1654
01:08:39,740 --> 01:08:42,034
Larry, we're the same age,
but you were always the teacher.

1655
01:08:42,118 --> 01:08:44,328
-No, Oscar. I...
-No, your whole career,

1656
01:08:44,411 --> 01:08:45,913
-you showed us the way.
-Well, I doubt...

1657
01:08:45,996 --> 01:08:47,665
You liberated us all.

1658
01:08:47,748 --> 01:08:49,792
You made American songs

1659
01:08:49,875 --> 01:08:51,418
finally sound
like American speech.

1660
01:08:51,502 --> 01:08:54,004
Stop. I dislike eulogies,
especially my own.

1661
01:08:54,088 --> 01:08:56,841
Oscar, tonight is your night.

1662
01:08:56,924 --> 01:09:00,553
A hit for you and Dick
is a hit for all of us.

1663
01:09:00,636 --> 01:09:03,222
People finally want
to go see shows again.

1664
01:09:03,305 --> 01:09:04,974
Real shows. Dick and I

1665
01:09:05,057 --> 01:09:06,225
were just tossing around
a few ideas.

1666
01:09:06,308 --> 01:09:08,018
-Wonderful.
-Yeah, we have this idea

1667
01:09:08,102 --> 01:09:09,728
for a big...

1668
01:09:10,980 --> 01:09:12,773
Marco Polo show... huge...

1669
01:09:12,857 --> 01:09:14,400
-Wow.
-...a kind of musical circus.

1670
01:09:14,483 --> 01:09:17,069
Dick's excited about it.

1671
01:09:17,153 --> 01:09:18,779
He said you're adapting Liliom.

1672
01:09:18,863 --> 01:09:20,531
Well, if we can
finagle the rights.

1673
01:09:20,614 --> 01:09:22,575
-Sounds exciting.
-Elizabeth told me

1674
01:09:22,658 --> 01:09:24,535
-she's your protégé.
-Stop.

1675
01:09:24,618 --> 01:09:27,037
"She's the promised kiss
of springtime

1676
01:09:27,121 --> 01:09:29,206
"that makes the lonely winter
seem long."

1677
01:09:29,290 --> 01:09:30,332
Oh, please.

1678
01:09:30,416 --> 01:09:32,418
You wrote a great lyric.

1679
01:09:32,501 --> 01:09:33,919
For a show
that didn't run three months.

1680
01:09:34,003 --> 01:09:36,172
-I saw it twice.
-You...

1681
01:09:36,255 --> 01:09:38,465
You know what we're talking
about here, Stevie?

1682
01:09:38,549 --> 01:09:40,885
Very Warm for May.
Produced by Max Gordon.

1683
01:09:40,968 --> 01:09:42,178
Music by Jerome Kern.

1684
01:09:42,261 --> 01:09:44,722
Alvin Theatre. 59 performances.

1685
01:09:44,805 --> 01:09:46,182
[BOTH LAUGH]

1686
01:09:46,265 --> 01:09:47,766
This is my neighbor.

1687
01:09:47,850 --> 01:09:50,477
He's a kind of a walking
encyclopedia of musical theater.

1688
01:09:50,561 --> 01:09:53,314
Well, I'm a kind of walking
pneumonia for musical theater.

1689
01:09:53,397 --> 01:09:55,357
-Nice to meet you.
-Pleasure to meet you.

1690
01:09:55,441 --> 01:09:57,109
He wants to write musicals
when he grows up.

1691
01:09:57,193 --> 01:09:58,861
Who's your favorite lyricist?

1692
01:09:58,944 --> 01:10:00,654
-Oscar.
-HART: Mmm-hmm.

1693
01:10:00,738 --> 01:10:02,907
Of course. And what do you think
of my work?

1694
01:10:02,990 --> 01:10:04,658
I like it. It's funny.

1695
01:10:04,742 --> 01:10:05,868
Thank you.

1696
01:10:05,951 --> 01:10:08,078
Can be a little sloppy at times.

1697
01:10:08,162 --> 01:10:10,456
OSCAR: He's very tired.
It's been a long day.

1698
01:10:10,539 --> 01:10:11,999
Come on, Stevie.
It's a long ride

1699
01:10:12,082 --> 01:10:13,667
back to Doylestown.

1700
01:10:13,751 --> 01:10:15,878
-You want to go to the bathroom?
-I'm not tired.

1701
01:10:15,961 --> 01:10:17,963
He asked me
what I thought of his work.

1702
01:10:18,047 --> 01:10:20,090
"Weighty affairs
will just have to wait"?

1703
01:10:20,174 --> 01:10:22,218
I so regret not having children.

1704
01:10:22,968 --> 01:10:25,429
-Cooper.
-[SIGHS]

1705
01:10:25,512 --> 01:10:27,056
I can't believe
I'm telling you this.

1706
01:10:27,139 --> 01:10:28,599
-Tell me.
-You have to remember,

1707
01:10:28,682 --> 01:10:29,892
I've had a hopeless crush
on this guy

1708
01:10:29,975 --> 01:10:31,060
for over a year.

1709
01:10:31,143 --> 01:10:33,896
