stuffed bears. [SCOFFS]

1959
01:21:51,615 --> 01:21:54,492
[SIGHING] Oh, Elizabeth.

1960
01:21:56,995 --> 01:21:58,121
Tell me truthfully.

1961
01:22:00,332 --> 01:22:01,791
How do you feel about me?

1962
01:22:05,128 --> 01:22:06,087
How do you mean?

1963
01:22:07,339 --> 01:22:08,298
I mean,

1964
01:22:09,466 --> 01:22:10,717
at the lake house,

1965
01:22:10,800 --> 01:22:12,594
with the light
burning off the water,

1966
01:22:14,304 --> 01:22:16,139
we talked
through half the night,

1967
01:22:17,515 --> 01:22:20,185
and I touched your shoulder...

1968
01:22:20,268 --> 01:22:22,604
Did you actually love me
a little bit that night?

1969
01:22:23,897 --> 01:22:25,482
You know I love you, Larry.

1970
01:22:28,193 --> 01:22:29,277
You do?

1971
01:22:32,739 --> 01:22:35,951
Just... not that way.

1972
01:22:44,250 --> 01:22:48,004
You said you'd drive 30 hours
to see Cooper.

1973
01:22:49,631 --> 01:22:52,884
How many hours would you drive
to see me?

1974
01:22:52,968 --> 01:22:55,512
Oh. [LAUGHS]
My hands are shaking.

1975
01:22:55,595 --> 01:22:57,263
[HART MUMBLES]

1976
01:22:57,347 --> 01:22:59,808
I feel something wiser
and deeper for you.

1977
01:22:59,891 --> 01:23:02,227
-Mmm. Thank you.
-Respect.

1978
01:23:02,310 --> 01:23:04,729
Gratitude for the generosity
and selflessness

1979
01:23:04,813 --> 01:23:06,356
you've shown me.

1980
01:23:06,439 --> 01:23:07,774
No one has ever been
more interested in my life

1981
01:23:07,857 --> 01:23:09,150
than you have.

1982
01:23:09,234 --> 01:23:11,277
I don't deserve
a friendship like yours.

1983
01:23:11,361 --> 01:23:12,821
I am so grateful for you.

1984
01:23:12,904 --> 01:23:15,198
I will be grateful forever.

1985
01:23:15,281 --> 01:23:17,784
I'm grateful to you, too,
for resurrecting me.

1986
01:23:17,867 --> 01:23:19,411
-[KNOCK ON DOOR]
-Stop.

1987
01:23:19,494 --> 01:23:20,704
RENEE: Larry!
There's people out here.

1988
01:23:20,787 --> 01:23:22,080
[SPUTTERS] One second!

1989
01:23:27,419 --> 01:23:30,547
-Can I ask you something?
-[CHUCKLES SOFTLY]

1990
01:23:30,630 --> 01:23:33,049
Something that's probably
too personal for me to ask?

1991
01:23:33,133 --> 01:23:36,177
Too personal is the only thing
that I'm interested in.

1992
01:23:36,970 --> 01:23:40,515
Uh... My mother...

1993
01:23:40,598 --> 01:23:41,683
You know,
she works for the Guild,

1994
01:23:41,766 --> 01:23:43,685
she knows everybody.

1995
01:23:43,768 --> 01:23:46,521
So she saw us spending time
together last summer...

1996
01:23:46,604 --> 01:23:49,691
I see where this is headed.

1997
01:23:49,774 --> 01:23:52,861
She said to me... I don't...
I don't know how to say this.

1998
01:23:52,944 --> 01:23:54,029
Just say it.

1999
01:23:56,322 --> 01:23:57,115
Okay.

2000
01:23:58,867 --> 01:24:00,869
She said to me, more than once,

2001
01:24:00,952 --> 01:24:04,706
"Elizabeth,
not only is he twice your age,

2002
01:24:04,789 --> 01:24:08,668
"but I don't think
his primary interest is women."

2003
01:24:08,752 --> 01:24:11,004
What did you say?

2004
01:24:11,087 --> 01:24:13,465
I said, "Look, Mother,
I know what I know.

2005
01:24:13,548 --> 01:24:15,175
"And I know what
we're both feeling."

2006
01:24:15,258 --> 01:24:17,093
Mmm-hmm.
What are we both feeling?

2007
01:24:21,639 --> 01:24:23,099
"Irrational adoration?"

2008
01:24:25,393 --> 01:24:28,229
And what may I ask
does your mother think

2009
01:24:28,313 --> 01:24:30,440
she knows
of my primary interest?

2010
01:24:31,816 --> 01:24:34,319
I'm sorry.
I shouldn't have said anything.

2011
01:24:35,320 --> 01:24:36,321
Please forgive me.

2012
01:24:37,822 --> 01:24:40,116
To all our mothers.

2013
01:24:40,200 --> 01:24:43,953
May they remain
mercifully silent.

2014
01:24:44,037 --> 01:24:46,289
I apologize. That was stupid
of me to have asked.

2015
01:24:46,372 --> 01:24:49,084
And I do know what I know,
and what I feel.

2016
01:24:49,167 --> 01:24:51,127
Here's what you tell
that mother of yours,

2017
01:24:51,211 --> 01:24:52,837
as she sits by the fire,

2018
01:24:52,921 --> 01:24:55,799
wistfully counting up
her royalties from Oklahoma!

2019
01:24:55,882 --> 01:24:59,302
You tell her that Larry Hart
is drunk with beauty.

2020
01:25:00,595 --> 01:25:03,223
And italicize the word "drunk."

2021
01:25:03,306 --> 01:25:05,975
Drunk with beauty,
wherever he finds it.

2022
01:25:06,059 --> 01:25:10,522
In men, in women,
in the smell of cigar stores,

2023
01:25:10,605 --> 01:25:15,360
in the impossible beauty
of a 20-year-old poet

2024
01:25:15,443 --> 01:25:17,362
with pale green eyes

2025
01:25:17,445 --> 01:25:20,115
and two tiny freckles
on her left cheek.

2026
01:25:24,869 --> 01:25:26,037
Are you all right?

2027
01:25:27,038 --> 01:25:29,666
It's... [INHALES DEEPLY]

2028
01:25:29,749 --> 01:25:31,668
-...my cigarette heart.
-[DOOR OPENS]

2029
01:25:31,751 --> 01:25:33,920
Larry, Dick Rodgers
is looking for his coat.

2030
01:25:34,003 --> 01:25:35,130
HART: Coming.

2031
01:25:52,105 --> 01:25:53,398
Introduce me?

2032
01:25:53,481 --> 01:25:56,568
[GRUNTING] Thank you, Renee.

2033
01:25:56,651 --> 01:25:59,404
Wonderful, thank you so much.

2034
01:25:59,487 --> 01:26:02,365
Dick, I want to introduce you
to my protégé,

2035
01:26:02,448 --> 01:26:03,575
Elizabeth Weiland.

2036
01:26:05,034 --> 01:26:06,161
Your protégé?

2037
01:26:07,078 --> 01:26:08,705
-Hello.
-Hello.

2038
01:26:08,788 --> 01:26:11,124
Well, you've got yourself
a damn good role model there

2039
01:26:11,207 --> 01:26:13,710
if you can stay away
from the bourbon.

2040
01:26:13,793 --> 01:26:18,214
Mmm. She's interested in writing
and set design and costumes.

2041
01:26:18,298 --> 01:26:19,799
Okay, really?

2042
01:26:19,883 --> 01:26:22,635
Mr. Rodgers, the show tonight,

2043
01:26:22,719 --> 01:26:24,220
I thought it was magnificent.

2044
01:26:24,304 --> 01:26:25,763
Well, thank you.

2045
01:26:25,847 --> 01:26:27,765
It was as perfect a musical show
as I've ever seen.

2046
01:26:27,849 --> 01:26:30,268
Well, that's
a lovely compliment.

2047
01:26:30,351 --> 01:26:32,437
Your mother's
with the Guild, am I right?

2048
01:26:32,520 --> 01:26:34,689
-Yes, Theresa Weiland.
-Yeah, Theresa, yeah.

2049
01:26:34,772 --> 01:26:36,399
Right, so you've seen
a lot of shows.

2050
01:26:36,482 --> 01:26:39,360
-Too many shows.
-[ALL LAUGHING]

2051
01:26:39,444 --> 01:26:40,445
Yeah.

2052
01:26:40,528 --> 01:26:41,988
I know what you mean.

2053
01:26:42,071 --> 01:26:43,406
But tonight what I saw was

2054
01:26:43,489 --> 01:26:44,908
what I'd always hoped
a show might be.

2055
01:26:44,991 --> 01:26:47,785
Uplifting. Smart. Magical.

2056
01:26:47,869 --> 01:26:50,079
Larry, this is the most
perceptive young woman

2057
01:26:50,163 --> 01:26:51,164
you've ever introduced me to.

2058
01:26:51,247 --> 01:26:52,707
[HART CHUCKLES]

2059
01:26:52,790 --> 01:26:54,209
-I've had a good teacher.
-HART: Mmm-hmm.

2060
01:26:55,084 --> 01:26:56,794
So, what do you write?

2061
01:26:56,878 --> 01:26:58,880
At this point, poetry mostly.

2062
01:26:58,963 --> 01:27:01,049
It's very strong.

2063
01:27:01,132 --> 01:27:02,842
And you do set design, too?

2064
01:27:02,926 --> 01:27:04,427
-I'm studying.
-RODGERS: Right.

2065
01:27:04,510 --> 01:27:08,348
Well, I'd love to...
read some of your poems.

2066
01:27:08,431 --> 01:27:10,934
Uh... See your sketches.
This is my...

2067
01:27:11,017 --> 01:27:13,228
This is my private number.

2068
01:27:14,854 --> 01:27:16,856
I'm flattered.

2069
01:27:16,940 --> 01:27:20,318
I haven't seen Dorothy around.
Is... Is... Is your wife here?

2070
01:27:20,401 --> 01:27:21,444
No, she's headed back
to the apartment.

2071
01:27:21,527 --> 01:27:22,737
We're all going to meet up there

2072
01:27:22,820 --> 01:27:24,572
to look at the late reviews,
Larry.

2073
01:27:24,656 --> 01:27:28,117
Yeah, so I'd love for you
to join us, Elizabeth.

2074
01:27:28,201 --> 01:27:29,494
It would be refreshing
to have someone

2075
01:27:29,577 --> 01:27:30,995
of the younger generation
amongst us.

2076
01:27:31,079 --> 01:27:33,122
And maybe you can
explain to me, Elizabeth,

2077
01:27:33,206 --> 01:27:36,084
why exactly the young girls
scream over Sinatra.

2078
01:27:36,167 --> 01:27:38,086
I don't scream over Sinatra.

2079
01:27:38,169 --> 01:27:39,963
No, she screams under Sinatra.

2080
01:27:40,046 --> 01:27:41,798
Okay, Larry!

2081
01:27:41,881 --> 01:27:43,466
[ALL LAUGHING]

2082
01:27:43,549 --> 01:27:45,677
Larry, of course,
you're welcome to come.

2083
01:27:45,760 --> 01:27:47,220
Oh, I'm going to try to make it,

2084
01:27:47,303 --> 01:27:49,472
I'm just throwing a little
soirée over at my place.

2085
01:27:49,555 --> 01:27:51,182
I've got the Golden Gate Quartet
coming...

2086
01:27:51,266 --> 01:27:54,769
Well, I can't compete with Larry
in the soirée department.

2087
01:27:54,852 --> 01:27:56,980
-I'd love to stop over.
-You would?

2088
01:27:57,063 --> 01:27:58,523
MAN: Dick, you coming?

2089
01:27:58,606 --> 01:27:59,941
Yep, yep.

2090
01:28:00,024 --> 01:28:01,442
Do you have a ride?

2091
01:28:01,526 --> 01:28:03,111
You know what,
I can take her in a cab later.

2092
01:28:03,194 --> 01:28:04,737
We're going right now
if you want to come with us?

2093
01:28:04,821 --> 01:28:06,281
-I'd love to.
-RODGERS: Great.

2094
01:28:07,240 --> 01:28:09,575
Just let me inform my mom.

2095
01:28:09,659 --> 01:28:11,411
The dutiful daughter.

2096
01:28:11,494 --> 01:28:12,328
[RODGERS CHUCKLES]

2097
01:28:14,414 --> 01:28:17,458
RODGERS: Wow. She is...
She is so lovely, right?

2098
01:28:18,835 --> 01:28:20,712
She's really striking.

2099
01:28:20,795 --> 01:28:23,172
Are you... Are you and she...

2100
01:28:23,256 --> 01:28:27,552
Oh, no, no.
I mean, I'm in love with her,

2101
01:28:27,635 --> 01:28:30,346
but, uh,
everybody's in love with her.

2102
01:28:31,806 --> 01:28:33,558
Just not that way.

2103
01:28:33,641 --> 01:28:34,475
Okay.

2104
01:28:38,771 --> 01:28:39,731
HART: Hmm.

2105
01:28:42,025 --> 01:28:42,859
[HART SIGHS SOFTLY]

2106
01:28:44,402 --> 01:28:45,820
So we'll see you
later on tonight?

2107
01:28:45,903 --> 01:28:47,071
Absolutely.

2108
01:28:49,073 --> 01:28:50,533
[CHUCKLES] Mmm-hmm.

2109
01:28:51,701 --> 01:28:52,744
Hmm.

2110
01:28:54,203 --> 01:28:55,330
Thank you for everything.

2111
01:28:57,040 --> 01:28:58,833
Mr. Rodgers.

2112
01:28:58,916 --> 01:29:00,084
Miss Weiland.

2113
01:29:02,086 --> 01:29:04,047
Thank you.

2114
01:29:04,130 --> 01:29:05,340
And we'll see you later,
Larry, right?

2115
01:29:09,260 --> 01:29:13,056
"With a song in my heart,
I behold your adorable face!"

2116
01:29:25,401 --> 01:29:27,779
[INHALES DEEPLY]

2117
01:29:35,620 --> 01:29:38,831
You ever meet someone,
and you know instantly

2118
01:29:38,915 --> 01:29:40,458
that both your lives

2119
01:29:40,541 --> 01:29:43,211
are going to be
irretrievably altered?

2120
01:29:43,294 --> 01:29:47,006
Just as I suspected.
You are a sentimentalist.

2121
01:29:47,090 --> 01:29:49,133
[HART LAUGHS]

2122
01:29:49,217 --> 01:29:50,259
When do you get off work?

2123
01:29:50,343 --> 01:29:52,053
I got to lock this joint up.

2124
01:29:52,136 --> 01:29:54,722
Ah, what about you, Knuckles,
you're done here, right?

2125
01:29:54,806 --> 01:29:56,516
Not yet.

2126
01:29:56,599 --> 01:29:58,643
Oh, 'cause I am throwing
an enormous party

2127
01:29:58,726 --> 01:29:59,852
at my place tonight.

2128
01:30:00,895 --> 01:30:03,106
[SIGHS]

2129
01:30:03,189 --> 01:30:04,816
I forgot to give her
the presents.

2130
01:30:06,275 --> 01:30:08,152
[SIGHS]

2131
01:30:08,236 --> 01:30:10,488
I'm having such trouble
with sleeves recently.

2132
01:30:10,571 --> 01:30:13,616
I have arthritis
in this shoulder.

2133
01:30:13,699 --> 01:30:16,077
I'm going to have to start
wearing a cloak.

2134
01:30:16,160 --> 01:30:18,121
I mean it. Single-handedly,

2135
01:30:18,204 --> 01:30:19,914
I'm going to bring back
the cloak.

2136
01:30:21,290 --> 01:30:22,417
[HART SIGHS]

2137
01:30:23,751 --> 01:30:25,294
[CLICKS TONGUE]

2138
01:30:25,378 --> 01:30:30,508
You're going to see me
on opening nights with a top hat

2139
01:30:30,591 --> 01:30:33,678
and a cloak,
flitting through the fog

2140
01:30:33,761 --> 01:30:36,139
with a silver-tipped cane.

2141
01:30:36,222 --> 01:30:38,433
You boys are
coming tonight, right?

2142
01:30:38,516 --> 01:30:42,061
She's going to be there.
I'm telling you, she will.

2143
01:30:42,145 --> 01:30:43,396
She'll arrive late.

2144
01:30:43,479 --> 01:30:45,022
And get this,

2145
01:30:45,106 --> 01:30:47,692
'cause this
is the remarkable part,

2146
01:30:47,775 --> 01:30:51,279
sometime late,
maybe around 5:00 a.m.,

2147
01:30:51,362 --> 01:30:53,698
after a little too much Malbec,

2148
01:30:53,781 --> 01:30:56,909
she's going to turn to me
and say,

2149
01:30:56,993 --> 01:31:00,163
"Hey, Larry,
what are you reading?

2150
01:31:00,246 --> 01:31:02,457
"Hey, Larry,
when's your birthday?"

2151
01:31:02,540 --> 01:31:05,334
For the first time in her life,

2152
01:31:05,418 --> 01:31:09,964
she's going to be
interested in me.

2153
01:31:10,047 --> 01:31:12,383
It has to happen sometime,
right?

2154
01:31:12,467 --> 01:31:14,552
All the other guys
have disappointed her.

2155
01:31:14,635 --> 01:31:18,681
But I never will. I'm just...

2156
01:31:18,764 --> 01:31:21,100
a little touch of Larry
in the night.

2157
01:31:22,768 --> 01:31:25,229
Knuckles. Some travelin' music.

2158
01:31:25,313 --> 01:31:27,315
[PLAYING PIANO]

2159
01:31:29,775 --> 01:31:30,610
MORTY:
♪ Blue moon ♪

2160
01:31:33,154 --> 01:31:36,365
♪ You saw me standing alone ♪

2161
01:31:37,033 --> 01:31:38,201
Wise guy.

2162
01:31:38,284 --> 01:31:41,370
♪ Without a dream in my heart ♪

2163
01:31:43,122 --> 01:31:46,000
♪ Without a love of my own ♪

2164
01:31:47,418 --> 01:31:49,170
Well, it's a helluva lot better

2165
01:31:49,253 --> 01:31:52,423
than The Surrey With
the Fucking Fringe On Top.

2166
01:31:52,507 --> 01:31:55,760
Larry, you coming uptown
to Dick's with us?

2167
01:31:55,843 --> 01:31:59,222
Oh, no, you go on ahead,
I'll be up there later.

2168
01:31:59,305 --> 01:32:00,431
MORTY AND RENEE:
♪ You knew just ♪

2169
01:32:00,515 --> 01:32:03,893
♪ What I was there for ♪

2170
01:32:03,976 --> 01:32:07,522
♪ You heard me
saying a prayer for ♪

2171
01:32:07,605 --> 01:32:09,148
[CHUCKLES SOFTLY]

2172
01:32:09,232 --> 01:32:12,109
♪ Someone I really
could care for ♪

2173
01:32:12,193 --> 01:32:13,819
That's not bad.

2174
01:32:13,903 --> 01:32:16,697
Triple feminine rhymes,
and they all make sense.

2175
01:32:16,781 --> 01:32:18,199
MORTY:
♪ And then suddenly ♪

2176
01:32:18,282 --> 01:32:20,743
♪ You appeared before me ♪

2177
01:32:20,826 --> 01:32:22,662
MORTY AND RENEE:
♪ The only one my arms ♪

2178
01:32:22,745 --> 01:32:25,289
♪ Will ever hold ♪

2179
01:32:25,373 --> 01:32:27,625
♪ And I heard
somebody whisper ♪

2180
01:32:27,708 --> 01:32:30,127
♪ Please adore me ♪

2181
01:32:30,211 --> 01:32:35,007
♪ And when I looked
The moon had turned to gold ♪

2182
01:32:35,091 --> 01:32:36,551
♪ Blue moon ♪

2183
01:32:44,058 --> 01:32:47,186
Larry, I think this
is the beginning

2184
01:32:47,270 --> 01:32:49,230
of a beautiful friendship.

2185
01:32:49,313 --> 01:32:50,565
"Isn't it romantic?"

2186
01:32:51,607 --> 01:32:52,608
Not really, no.

2187
01:32:56,862 --> 01:32:58,281
You know, it wasn't even

2188
01:32:58,364 --> 01:33:00,283
supposed to be
called Blue Moon.

2189
01:33:00,366 --> 01:33:03,869
I called it The Bad
in Every Man.

2190
01:33:03,953 --> 01:33:06,706
We wrote it for this movie,
Manhattan Melodrama.

2191
01:33:06,789 --> 01:33:08,583
Jack Robbins,
formerly "Rabinowitz,"

2192
01:33:08,666 --> 01:33:10,710
he had his name circumcised
over at MGM,

2193
01:33:10,793 --> 01:33:12,461
he hears the song,
he calls us into his office,

2194
01:33:12,545 --> 01:33:14,297
he lowers his pastrami, says...

2195
01:33:14,380 --> 01:33:17,425
[IN NASAL VOICE]
"Dick, I love the melody.

2196
01:33:17,508 --> 01:33:20,011
"Strong as anything
you've ever written.

2197
01:33:20,094 --> 01:33:24,307
"But Larry, those words,
they're too artsy-fartsy!

2198
01:33:24,390 --> 01:33:25,891
"They're not too
artsy-fartsy for me.

2199
01:33:25,975 --> 01:33:27,935
"I know how brilliant
you guys are.

2200
01:33:28,019 --> 01:33:30,396
"But you have to write
for the schmucks in the dark.

2201
01:33:30,479 --> 01:33:32,023
"Nobody's going
to sing that lyric."

2202
01:33:32,106 --> 01:33:33,816
[IN NORMAL VOICE] So I said,
"What's wrong with it?"

2203
01:33:33,899 --> 01:33:34,817
He said... [IN NASAL VOICE]
"I can't even

2204
01:33:34,900 --> 01:33:36,694
"remember the fucking title!

2205
01:33:36,777 --> 01:33:39,447
"Boys, give me
something I can promote."

2206
01:33:39,530 --> 01:33:40,698
[IN NORMAL VOICE] So I say,

2207
